/worked-solution "To plot the given function, we follow these steps:\n\n- We take the curve $y={(\\frac{2}{3})}^{x}.$\n\n![](images/fb/fbbddeb527a9ea78b274fd7c4afe9809546a61ba833a67a385cb18aac9e14942.png)\n\n \n\n- Horizontally shift it by $1$ unit to the right to give $y={(\\frac{2}{3})}^{x-1}.$ The $y$-intercept moves from $(0,1)$ to $(1,1)$ but the horizontal asymptote stays at $y=0.$\n\n![](images/61/617dbba0d17c30ce03da929550d17062fd644ed7992a07b55caa02e5815e7232.png)\n\n- Then, reflect it on the $x$-axis to give $y=-{(\\frac{2}{3})}^{x-1}.$ The point $(1,1)$ moves to $(1,-1)$ and the horizontal asymptote stays at $y=0.$\n\n![](images/7e/7e4d38e401c4ce01330d6082dadd43e50974a55b666023d8b8d51bbcbc7f7258.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186390687 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186217153 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b4b5807f-a10a-518a-84fa-907bf699884c" :knowledge-point/title "Exponential Functions With Vertical Reflections and Horizontal Shifts"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cc9b1f7f-77e0-5da3-a182-cc99609a2934" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/f5/f5643bb8e251468bea69940e4c0335739096d371e36ab569d898b8675da5f3a0.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390689} {:answer/id #uuid "bb991bf3-446e-5307-9c14-0e8ab3fa5b02" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/1a/1ab4f9a548004379156deb2f1e152a16b88b585ee7d576674d394cf96c5bca20.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390690} {:answer/id #uuid "0e9da463-d94e-51f1-810f-5dd1f81b36f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/2f/2fddab60da44e86e5c99f9f2933145602a1aa8660140488a9ce2da42aab5900a.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390691} {:answer/id #uuid "8f17d8a1-3239-5d48-8787-3f1e717b189e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/51/51b6901035dee1fe0ba56eb7b06b1ca0d43a1edf612cfff23ea44383b8bdd312.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390692} {:answer/id #uuid "9cfd6fee-10fa-50ff-9481-7390456eb2fc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/d9/d968dcdb24e3e5538ccbe51ec4c2a94b8f8e4b23b585810a3d24fcbf255e15ed.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390693}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8f17d8a1-3239-5d48-8787-3f1e717b189e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/51/51b6901035dee1fe0ba56eb7b06b1ca0d43a1edf612cfff23ea44383b8bdd312.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390692} :answer-field/id #uuid "b2555f44-ce03-5e69-bf96-d85a58e09bb8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390688}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8a41594a-f79c-59b8-b328-4364bfa01e24" :question/math-academy-id "q-317898" :question/problem "Which of the following is the graph of  $y=-{(\\frac{3}{10})}^{x+1}?$" :question/worked-solution "To plot the given function, we follow these steps:\n\n- We take the curve $y={(\\frac{3}{10})}^{x}.$\n\n![](images/a9/a9b6c2b12c1bc4bc7794dd5d409a99c61bf256ea58f9a016657cbbea5ef6b1e4.png)\n\n \n\n- Horizontally shift it by $1$ units to the left to give $y={(\\frac{3}{10})}^{x+1}.$ The $y$-intercept moves from $(0,1)$ to $(-1,1)$ but the horizontal asymptote stays at $y=0.$\n\n![](images/5f/5f4c0e3c4573f6e3167390d54ee21bbb8bf6e3879fcc06e7adf9d311e80ccb62.png)\n\n- Then, reflect it on the $x$-axis to give $y=-{(\\frac{3}{10})}^{x+1}.$ The point $(-1,1)$ moves to $(-1,-1)$ and the horizontal asymptote stays at $y=0.$\n\n![](images/b5/b541282b8661f2fb28d0ce1f10c22a9a8cc7b083460c05093a89722bc084d7b9.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115731 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115249 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b7f42188-bf51-5546-89fb-e33ba47c4b5e" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Row Operations Required To Find the Inverse of a 2x2 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1452f7af-82c6-55ad-851f-0af4df86350c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{R}_{2}:=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{2},\\,{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}-2{R}_{1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398695} {:answer/id #uuid "fe1a813f-c908-5653-a64d-7211791782ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{R}_{2}:=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{2},\\,{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398696} {:answer/id #uuid "481208ae-3c57-5f00-939f-d1f24b8106ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{R}_{2}↔{R}_{2},\\,{R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+2{R}_{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398697} {:answer/id #uuid "d3ba0c9a-7588-50be-bba0-9e1ec3e66758" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{R}_{2}:=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{2},\\,{R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+2{R}_{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398698} {:answer/id #uuid "c0d4923d-606c-58ee-8c6b-a4ff5482ef83" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{R}_{2}↔{R}_{2},\\,{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}-\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398699}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d3ba0c9a-7588-50be-bba0-9e1ec3e66758" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{R}_{2}:=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{2},\\,{R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+2{R}_{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398698} :answer-field/id #uuid "d60d7e8d-d42b-5c16-b699-93d0d7f45a76" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398694}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5202eb69-24ce-5ca6-bebd-52036979fd02" :question/math-academy-id "q-31862" :question/problem "The inverse of the matrix $A$ is found using row operations, as shown below. What are the row operations corresponding to each step?\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[A\\,|\\,I] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] &  & \\text{___________} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & \\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}] &  & \\text{___________} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & \\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We write down $A$ and the identity matrix $I$ side-by-side (as shown below) and apply the standard Gauss-Jordan method to transform the matrix $A$ into the identity matrix. We perform the same row operations on both matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[A\\,|\\,I] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & \\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+2{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & \\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding row operations are\n\n$$\n{R}_{2}:=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{2},\\,{R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+2{R}_{2}.\n$$\n\nThe matrix ${A}^{-1}$ is given by the right-hand side of our big matrix after all of the transformations have been completed. Therefore, \n\n$$\n{A}^{-1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}].\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299700 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115250 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8a4b135f-df0d-5443-bb93-796f32c380c6" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Inverse of a 2x2 Matrix Calculated Using Row Operations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3a987cf7-3d87-5d25-bd5c-dd880b1daa91" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{B}^{-1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ -\\frac{3}{5} & \\frac{1}{5}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297782} {:answer/id #uuid "96bd62c8-0cca-5580-ae04-416b82048473" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{B}^{-1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ \\frac{3}{5} & -\\frac{1}{5}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298206} {:answer/id #uuid "bf3c5dd7-085b-57d7-9575-a9f3f0bf6441" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{B}^{-1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -\\frac{3}{5} & \\frac{1}{5}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298392} {:answer/id #uuid "c817c443-b57e-5f24-8265-b649a1f3ac77" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{B}^{-1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{3}{5} & \\frac{1}{5}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298426} {:answer/id #uuid "d1bc9edf-5bdb-5311-bb9b-39a82ba608ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{B}^{-1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{3}{5} & -\\frac{1}{5}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298460}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bf3c5dd7-085b-57d7-9575-a9f3f0bf6441" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{B}^{-1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -\\frac{3}{5} & \\frac{1}{5}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298392} :answer-field/id #uuid "a66018ec-422c-5e0e-b376-357aef68a84e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299198}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d5898f79-8d87-5e96-a0ad-da8516834a84" :question/math-academy-id "q-31871" :question/problem "The inverse of the $2\\times 2$ matrix $B$ is found using row operations, as shown below. Complete the process, and determine ${B}^{-1}.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[B\\,|\\,I\\,] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 5 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-3){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5 & -3 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :=\\text{___________} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 &  &  \\\\ 0 & 1 &  & \\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We write down $B$ and the identity matrix $I$ side-by-side (as shown below) and apply the standard Gauss-Jordan method to transform the matrix $B$ into the identity matrix. We perform the same row operations on both matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[B\\,|\\,I\\,] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 5 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-3){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5 & -3 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :=\\frac{1}{5}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -\\frac{3}{5} & \\frac{1}{5}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe matrix ${B}^{-1}$ is given by the right-hand side of our big matrix after all of the transformations have been completed. Therefore, \n\n$$\n{B}^{-1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -\\frac{3}{5} & \\frac{1}{5}\\end{aligned}].\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401981 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115249 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b7f42188-bf51-5546-89fb-e33ba47c4b5e" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Row Operations Required To Find the Inverse of a 2x2 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "491969ed-8b76-5f12-8e92-e2c07f40a5f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{R}_{2}:=\\frac{1}{5}{R}_{2},\\,{R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+2{R}_{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401983} {:answer/id #uuid "47e753ff-b001-5c13-a3e5-fa0b1469d565" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{R}_{2}:=\\frac{1}{3}{R}_{2},\\,{R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+\\frac{5}{2}{R}_{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401984} {:answer/id #uuid "0f0a1d9a-26c8-5f3e-8675-24c9ff948a9f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{R}_{2}:=3{R}_{2},\\,{R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+5{R}_{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401985} {:answer/id #uuid "345a93b2-c8e2-542e-9732-fd3c08c01fef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{R}_{2}:=\\frac{1}{3}{R}_{2},\\,{R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+\\frac{1}{3}{R}_{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401986} {:answer/id #uuid "80637310-e8ef-5a17-b8ea-c4cf7c2aacb4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{R}_{2}:=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{2},\\,{R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+\\frac{5}{2}{R}_{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401987}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "47e753ff-b001-5c13-a3e5-fa0b1469d565" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{R}_{2}:=\\frac{1}{3}{R}_{2},\\,{R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+\\frac{5}{2}{R}_{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401984} :answer-field/id #uuid "f08b9f41-8719-5f37-ae27-999f6560f9f4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401982}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "0ac0783b-5b73-5c8c-959b-6029e5d56c68" :question/math-academy-id "q-31872" :question/problem "The inverse of the matrix $J$ is found using row operations, as shown below. What are the row operations corresponding to the blank spaces?\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[J\\,|\\,I] & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -5 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -\\frac{5}{2} & \\frac{1}{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] &  & \\text{___________} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -\\frac{5}{2} & \\frac{1}{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & \\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}] &  & \\text{___________} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & \\frac{1}{2} & \\frac{5}{6} \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & \\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We write down $J$ and the identity matrix $I$ side-by-side (as shown below) and apply the standard Gauss-Jordan method to transform the matrix $J$ into the identity matrix. We perform the same row operations on both matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[J\\,|\\,I] & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -5 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -\\frac{5}{2} & \\frac{1}{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :=\\frac{1}{3}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -\\frac{5}{2} & \\frac{1}{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & \\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+\\frac{5}{2}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & \\frac{1}{2} & \\frac{5}{6} \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & \\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding row operations are\n\n$$\n{R}_{1}:=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1},\\,{R}_{2}:=\\frac{1}{3}{R}_{2},\\,{R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+\\frac{5}{2}{R}_{2}.\n$$\n\nThe matrix ${J}^{-1}$ is given by the right-hand side of our big matrix after all of the transformations have been completed. Therefore, \n\n$$\n{J}^{-1}=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2} & \\frac{5}{6} \\\\ 0 & \\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}].\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401988 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115236 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "64b2036f-da0f-59de-8a39-6b33e6cbdd04" :knowledge-point/title "Completing a True Statement"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "26bd5bb7-0a8a-5edb-975d-4fd5f046599e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401990} {:answer/id #uuid "08992256-6bf5-5ba6-a198-d2f970ef2ab7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401991} {:answer/id #uuid "52bbf42f-38cf-5d5c-af4e-ee00925fa061" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401992} {:answer/id #uuid "61cc3875-ca37-5eff-9f67-f6c05f654183" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401993} {:answer/id #uuid "5af54dbb-f852-501b-bb4f-1e25a922f13c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401994}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "08992256-6bf5-5ba6-a198-d2f970ef2ab7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401991} :answer-field/id #uuid "b3cd4133-bfb2-57ca-9d13-24b0af1c763d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401989}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "561c404c-366e-5e58-b3aa-1094d48ff6e7" :question/math-academy-id "q-31919" :question/problem "Which of the following completes the statement so that it is true:\n\n*If $det(A)\\ne 0,$ where $A$ is a $2\\times 2$-matrix, then $A$ is $\\text{__________}$ .*\n\nI. invertible\nII. **not ** invertible\nIII. singular" :question/worked-solution "Recall that given $A=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22}\\end{aligned}]$, then according to the invertible matrix theorem the following statements are equivalent:    \n\n- $A$ is invertible.\n\n- The system $Ax=b$ has exactly one solution for any $2\\times 1$ column vector $b.$\n\n- The system $Ax=0$ has the unique zero solution.\n\n- The lines ${a}_{11}x+{a}_{12}y={b}_{1}$ and ${a}_{21}x+{a}_{22}y={b}_{2}$ intersect at exactly one point.\n\n- $det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now check our statements.  \n\nNow, since the fifth part of the invertible matrix theorem is true ($det(A)\\ne 0$), all the other parts of the theorem are true too. Therefore, according to the theorem, only option I gives a true statement:\n\n- *If $det(A)\\ne 0$ , then $A$ is invertible.* $\\,✓$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401995 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115249 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b7f42188-bf51-5546-89fb-e33ba47c4b5e" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Row Operations Required To Find the Inverse of a 2x2 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f4eea07e-f2f3-5991-9f8c-d1a3d485cb8f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+4{R}_{1},\\,{R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+4{R}_{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401997} {:answer/id #uuid "b42745fe-be44-51aa-b6af-8f31a15a57a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1},\\,{R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+\\frac{3}{4}{R}_{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401998} {:answer/id #uuid "428db092-3f21-5636-9fa7-75f2fd6123d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1},\\,{R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+(-3){R}_{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401999} {:answer/id #uuid "8f69e7f4-02bf-5a3c-99ee-ec0db4ec6abb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1},\\,{R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+\\frac{1}{3}{R}_{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402000} {:answer/id #uuid "3e91bc4e-4104-5e61-9a68-1d8607d11d41" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1},\\,{R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+\\frac{4}{3}{R}_{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402001}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3e91bc4e-4104-5e61-9a68-1d8607d11d41" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1},\\,{R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+\\frac{4}{3}{R}_{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402001} :answer-field/id #uuid "b2cc2c12-ff60-5e41-a718-fde30b82b390" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401996}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "d8e64088-2308-5369-8285-5d893b0dfcdf" :question/math-academy-id "q-31945" :question/problem "The inverse of the matrix $K$ is found using row operations, as shown below. What are the row operations corresponding to the blank spaces?\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[K\\,|\\,I] & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 4 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 3 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :=-\\frac{1}{3}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -\\frac{4}{3} & -\\frac{1}{3} & 0 \\\\ -2 & 3 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] &  & \\text{___________} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -\\frac{4}{3} & -\\frac{1}{3} & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{1}{3} & -\\frac{2}{3} & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :=3{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -\\frac{4}{3} & -\\frac{1}{3} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 & 3\\end{aligned}] &  & \\text{___________} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -3 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 & 3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We write down $K$ and the identity matrix $I$ side-by-side (as shown below) and apply the standard Gauss-Jordan method to transform the matrix $K$ into the identity matrix. We perform the same row operations on both matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[K\\,|\\,I] & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 4 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 3 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :=-\\frac{1}{3}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -\\frac{4}{3} & -\\frac{1}{3} & 0 \\\\ -2 & 3 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -\\frac{4}{3} & -\\frac{1}{3} & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{1}{3} & -\\frac{2}{3} & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :=3{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -\\frac{4}{3} & -\\frac{1}{3} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 & 3\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+\\frac{4}{3}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -3 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 & 3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding row operations are\n\n$$\n{R}_{1}:=-\\frac{1}{3}{R}_{1},\\,{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1},\\,{R}_{2}:=3{R}_{2},\\,{R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+\\frac{4}{3}{R}_{2}.\n$$\n\nThe matrix ${K}^{-1}$ is given by the right-hand side of our big matrix after all of the transformations have been completed. Therefore, \n\n$$\n{K}^{-1}=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 4 \\\\ -2 & 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299772 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115401 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4c1def23-83e0-5dc7-b20b-9693127d3a9e" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating Inverse Reciprocal Trigonometric Functions Using the Chain Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "01348264-a81e-56c3-895f-616259a8e027" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{6}{|6x-1|\\sqrt{9{x}^{2}-3x}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297556} {:answer/id #uuid "0394f679-9a1c-5b87-94ed-5055b0ccff99" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6}{|6x-1|\\sqrt{9{x}^{2}-3x}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297572} {:answer/id #uuid "50c36e45-828c-545f-9d7e-4d60c934349c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6}{|6x-1|\\sqrt{3{x}^{2}-x}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297888} {:answer/id #uuid "5a70b39e-a686-576d-b119-facfb9478e8f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{|6x-1|\\sqrt{3{x}^{2}-x}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297932} {:answer/id #uuid "fe851005-bb92-50a4-8f77-18e97f095b87" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{|6x-1|\\sqrt{9{x}^{2}-3x}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298643}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0394f679-9a1c-5b87-94ed-5055b0ccff99" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6}{|6x-1|\\sqrt{9{x}^{2}-3x}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297572} :answer-field/id #uuid "29c39276-107a-53e2-88ed-bb72cf785946" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298779}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1b0ce731-e82d-557a-b699-13d5ea673ea4" :question/math-academy-id "q-31951" :question/problem "Find ${f}^{′}(x)$ if $f(x)=2arcsec(6x-1).$" :question/worked-solution "The formula for the derivative of $arcsec$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(arcsecx)=\\frac{1}{|x|\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1}}.\n$$\n\nUsing the chain rule and the formula above, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2arcsec(6x-1)) \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(arcsec(6x-1)) \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{1}{|6x-1|\\sqrt{(6x-1)^{2}-1}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(6x-1) \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{1}{|6x-1|\\sqrt{{(6x-1)}^{2}-1}}\\cdot 6 \\\\  & =\\frac{12}{|6x-1|\\sqrt{36{x}^{2}-12x}} \\\\  & =\\frac{12}{|6x-1|\\sqrt{4(9{x}^{2}-3x)}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{|6x-1|\\sqrt{9{x}^{2}-3x}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299806 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078459 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fb183013-a42d-50a3-a1b8-cd8501781075" :knowledge-point/title "Factoring a Polynomial With Multiple Variables as a Difference of Squares"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "17f420e0-2ce0-5aca-8dee-2fc02f4be838" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "({x}^{2}+{y}^{3})({x}^{2}-{y}^{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297656} {:answer/id #uuid "2decc769-5d34-52a4-8f61-0d917cf2c0e6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "({x}^{5}+{y}^{2})(x-{y}^{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297746} {:answer/id #uuid "84d3cf7b-8bb1-5c05-bad0-4556bf860b32" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "({x}^{3}+{y}^{3})({x}^{2}-{y}^{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298125} {:answer/id #uuid "903c4fc8-2bfb-5749-a8dc-9a4f1681fb2d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "({x}^{3}+{y}^{2})({x}^{3}-{y}^{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298178} {:answer/id #uuid "bd0ab2a9-e83d-55df-8e55-44c8b207e2a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})({x}^{2}-{y}^{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298375}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "17f420e0-2ce0-5aca-8dee-2fc02f4be838" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "({x}^{2}+{y}^{3})({x}^{2}-{y}^{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297656} :answer-field/id #uuid "710ade4f-6788-57df-bfae-27eab8eaa863" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299022}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a03ef93a-a766-5bc2-9cd5-d8ee9f70bca4" :question/math-academy-id "q-31991" :question/problem "Factor ${x}^{4}-{y}^{6}.$" :question/worked-solution "The difference of squares formula is given by\n\n$$\n{a}^{2}-{b}^{2}=(a+b)(a-b)\n$$\n\nNotice that our expression can be written as a difference of squares:\n\n$$\n{x}^{4}-{y}^{6}={({x}^{2})}^{2}-{({y}^{3})}^{2}\n$$\n\nFinally, we factor our expression using the difference of squares formula:\n\n$$\n{({x}^{2})}^{2}-{({y}^{3})}^{2}=({x}^{2}+{y}^{3})({x}^{2}-{y}^{3})\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115739 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115401 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4c1def23-83e0-5dc7-b20b-9693127d3a9e" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating Inverse Reciprocal Trigonometric Functions Using the Chain Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "006c4d37-009e-5065-aa0e-e789bc12caf9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{4{x}^{3}}{|8-{x}^{4}|\\sqrt{(8-{x}^{4})^{2}-1}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297551} {:answer/id #uuid "a3a34a37-aaa2-5b33-8413-e4f969d084a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{12{x}^{3}}{|8-{x}^{4}|\\sqrt{(8-{x}^{4})^{2}-1}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298270} {:answer/id #uuid "bb221971-81aa-5b2d-a077-c6b670f32b1b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8{x}^{3}}{|8-{x}^{4}|\\sqrt{(8-{x}^{4})^{2}-1}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298367} {:answer/id #uuid "c0e98b1b-1585-5bcb-a6c7-f9e80aef1351" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6{x}^{3}}{|8-{x}^{4}|\\sqrt{(8-{x}^{4})^{2}-1}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298401} {:answer/id #uuid "cd44316a-87ed-548b-af75-38a85154bbc0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{12{x}^{3}}{|8-{x}^{4}|\\sqrt{(8-{x}^{4})^{2}-1}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298447}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bb221971-81aa-5b2d-a077-c6b670f32b1b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8{x}^{3}}{|8-{x}^{4}|\\sqrt{(8-{x}^{4})^{2}-1}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298367} :answer-field/id #uuid "b39d87eb-5482-5669-84b6-1f25fa680a00" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299244}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c5f8a50f-d033-5259-86d9-bade982764d7" :question/math-academy-id "q-32000" :question/problem "Calculate ${f}^{′}(x)$ for $f(x)=2arccsc(8-{x}^{4}).$" :question/worked-solution "The formula for the derivative of $\\text{arccsc}$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\text{arccsc}\\,x)=-\\frac{1}{|x|\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1}}.\n$$\n\nUsing the chain rule and the formula above, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2arccsc(8-{x}^{4})) \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(arccsc(8-{x}^{4})) \\\\  & =2(-\\frac{1}{|8-{x}^{4}|\\sqrt{(8-{x}^{4})^{2}-1}})\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(8-{x}^{4}) \\\\  & =2(-\\frac{1}{|8-{x}^{4}|\\sqrt{(8-{x}^{4})^{2}-1}})\\cdot (-4{x}^{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{8{x}^{3}}{|8-{x}^{4}|\\sqrt{(8-{x}^{4})^{2}-1}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186390694 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092183 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c89778fa-3c8d-5f0a-a0f8-c9b3d9193ffd" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a 2x2 System of Equations Using an Inverse Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ad3e10c2-e2f1-58e8-b2f9-00f2c4c2df6f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{1}{31}[\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 \\\\ 8 & 5\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390696} {:answer/id #uuid "30e4ebf6-13b6-590d-9607-b5715eb73e24" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & -2 \\\\ 8 & -5\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390697} {:answer/id #uuid "7aac971d-5512-509f-afcf-e37b9c5bd0a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-\\frac{1}{31}[\\begin{aligned}5 & 2 \\\\ -8 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390698} {:answer/id #uuid "de4d0df2-2d46-543e-b9bd-33d2e628c41a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{1}{31}[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 \\\\ -8 & 5\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390699} {:answer/id #uuid "496d8420-4ee1-5bb7-a84d-0f998d3229fc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 2 \\\\ -8 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390700}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "de4d0df2-2d46-543e-b9bd-33d2e628c41a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{1}{31}[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 \\\\ -8 & 5\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390699} :answer-field/id #uuid "23bd791b-3244-5621-8d76-a43da7beeff2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390695}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c60618eb-8102-5d29-9fef-37f3fce33d0e" :question/math-academy-id "q-32026" :question/problem "Which of the following represents the solution to the system of linear equations given below?\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}5x-2y=1 \\\\ 8x+3y=0\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "This system can be written as\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}5 & -2 \\\\ 8 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo find the solution, we need to calculate the inverse of the coefficient matrix \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}5 & -2 \\\\ 8 & 3\\end{aligned}].$\nThis gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{5\\cdot 3-(-2)\\cdot 8}[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 \\\\ -8 & 5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{31}[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 \\\\ -8 & 5\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now represent the solution to this system, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={A}^{-1}b \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{31}[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 \\\\ -8 & 5\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115744 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115234 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d49a7b78-bea1-54e5-8232-3fea0d4eb5a3" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a True Statement Concerning a 2x2 System of Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "11c21c6d-1733-55e6-ae62-ff9455761bba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297633} {:answer/id #uuid "39ec9bc9-bb09-5cab-b8c8-16ed6e2d2ba1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297779} {:answer/id #uuid "9dffde52-21e5-5601-b349-6c8d7ff77873" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298244} {:answer/id #uuid "cc4e1a09-9e67-57f1-8045-c8a0276fa706" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298439} {:answer/id #uuid "dd29ba58-48e3-5699-abf9-17ba99e9924b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298500}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "39ec9bc9-bb09-5cab-b8c8-16ed6e2d2ba1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297779} :answer-field/id #uuid "cf059d7e-0a61-50de-b3d7-bd32d0f212a7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299334}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "947396a7-1dd0-5962-800b-c42f03f930a3" :question/math-academy-id "q-32091" :question/problem "Consider the system of linear equations given by\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}3x+\\sqrt{7}y=0.3 \\\\ -2\\sqrt{0.1}x+3y=\\sqrt{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven that the system has exactly one solution, which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The lines $3x+\\sqrt{7}y=0.3$ and $-2\\sqrt{0.1}x+3y=\\sqrt{7}$ intersect exactly at one point\n- The determinant of the coefficient matrix is equal to $0$\n- The coefficient matrix is invertible" :question/worked-solution "Recall that given $A=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22}\\end{aligned}]$, then according to the invertible matrix theorem the following statements are equivalent:    \n\n- $A$ is invertible.\n\n- The system $Ax=b$ has exactly one solution for any $2\\times 1$ column vector $b.$\n\n- The system $Ax=0$ has the unique zero solution.\n\n- The lines ${a}_{11}x+{a}_{12}y={b}_{1}$ and ${a}_{21}x+{a}_{22}y={b}_{2}$ intersect at exactly one point.\n\n- $det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\nAlso, we are told that the second part of the invertible matrix theorem is true. So, all the other parts of the theorem are true too. Therefore, according to the theorem:\n\n- Statement I is true by the fourth part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\n- Statement II is false. According to the fifth part of the invertible matrix theorem, the determinant cannot be $0.$\n\n- Statement III is true by the first part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\nTherefore, only statements I and III are true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186398700 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115236 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "64b2036f-da0f-59de-8a39-6b33e6cbdd04" :knowledge-point/title "Completing a True Statement"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a7c8774e-9109-5016-afc5-bf50bfde4b21" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "det[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 1 \\\\ k & -1\\end{aligned}]=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398702} {:answer/id #uuid "6da42ba2-60ea-55ee-a8cc-e05327ecf3a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ is the unique solution of $[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 1 \\\\ k & -1\\end{aligned}]x=0$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398703} {:answer/id #uuid "e5c547a0-c335-58ec-ab07-57aced1b8ae4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "det[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 1 \\\\ k & -1\\end{aligned}]\\ne 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398704} {:answer/id #uuid "dc5a5fd6-f1c0-5816-bbff-d97051dd254d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 1 \\\\ k & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]$ has exactly one solution" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398705} {:answer/id #uuid "404e1d93-c357-5c09-b96b-df966f10e2b3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 1 \\\\ k & -1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}$ is invertible" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398706}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a7c8774e-9109-5016-afc5-bf50bfde4b21" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "det[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 1 \\\\ k & -1\\end{aligned}]=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398702} :answer-field/id #uuid "a491e86e-1311-5936-86d4-f5c880062fc1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398701}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "d83ee387-80b0-5c7f-bd0c-361b0164d684" :question/math-academy-id "q-32092" :question/problem "Consider the lines $y-5x=-1$ and $kx-y=-3$, where $k$ is a constant. Complete the following statement so that it is true:\n\n*If the lines don't intersect, then $\\text{__________}\\,.$*" :question/worked-solution "Recall that given $A=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22}\\end{aligned}]$, then according to the invertible matrix theorem the following statements are equivalent:    \n\n- $A$ is invertible.\n\n- The system $Ax=b$ has exactly one solution for any $2\\times 1$ column vector $b.$\n\n- The system $Ax=0$ has the unique zero solution.\n\n- The lines ${a}_{11}x+{a}_{12}y={b}_{1}$ and ${a}_{21}x+{a}_{22}y={b}_{2}$ intersect at exactly one point.\n\n- $det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now check our statements.\n\nAccording to the invertible matrix theorem, if the lines $y-5x=-1$ and $kx-y=-3$ intersect exactly at one point, then the matrix\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 1 \\\\ k & -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nis invertible and $det(A)\\ne 0.$ \nAdditionally, the unique solution to $Ax=0$ is $⟨0,0⟩$ and\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 1 \\\\ k & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}{b}_{1} \\\\ {b}_{2}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nhas exactly one solution  for any $2\\times 1$ column vector $b.$  \n\nTherefore, the true statement is as follows:\n\n *If the lines **don't** intersect, then $det[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 1 \\\\ k & -1\\end{aligned}]=0.$*"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186390711 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092183 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c89778fa-3c8d-5f0a-a0f8-c9b3d9193ffd" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a 2x2 System of Equations Using an Inverse Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b2cf0dd5-0db7-57a2-9870-1611db6da665" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390713} {:answer/id #uuid "be6ce68f-4c42-58da-863b-b22d6376ce30" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & -1 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390714} {:answer/id #uuid "333a570b-447e-5d5d-9439-65c7fcbb64c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-\\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390715} {:answer/id #uuid "ae5e534f-499c-5a4d-9bd0-1ecbfeee8157" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390716} {:answer/id #uuid "f518abcf-bf8c-56aa-bf15-d5e00e0d770d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390717}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ae5e534f-499c-5a4d-9bd0-1ecbfeee8157" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390716} :answer-field/id #uuid "89bea882-0921-5708-a9f9-9d676afb881e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390712}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ffd8425a-ff52-5722-b741-7d19394751e5" :question/math-academy-id "q-32136" :question/problem "Which of the following represents the solution to the system of linear equations given below?\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x-y=-1 \\\\ 2x+3y=0\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "This system can be written as\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo find the solution, we need to calculate the inverse of the coefficient matrix \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}].$\nThis gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{1\\cdot 3-(-1)\\cdot 2}[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now represent the solution to this system, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={A}^{-1}b \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186390718 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092183 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c89778fa-3c8d-5f0a-a0f8-c9b3d9193ffd" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a 2x2 System of Equations Using an Inverse Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "af53f340-3e36-5545-937e-11e307962671" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-\\frac{1}{50}[\\begin{aligned}2 & -6 \\\\ -8 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}46 \\\\ -23\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390720} {:answer/id #uuid "f3ec4ea9-2509-5c2c-a216-2db5287d79fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-\\frac{1}{46}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -8 \\\\ -6 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}46 \\\\ -23\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390721} {:answer/id #uuid "99392c81-e8f0-5fb5-ae4c-99f2a069feb9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{1}{50}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 6 \\\\ 8 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}46 \\\\ -23\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390722} {:answer/id #uuid "bff6331c-295a-540c-a6d8-28d96895301d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{1}{46}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 6 \\\\ 8 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}46 \\\\ -23\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390723} {:answer/id #uuid "4d16e9a1-1407-53d0-b940-8956dc7a7aa2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-\\frac{1}{46}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -6 \\\\ -8 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}46 \\\\ -23\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390724}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4d16e9a1-1407-53d0-b940-8956dc7a7aa2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-\\frac{1}{46}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -6 \\\\ -8 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}46 \\\\ -23\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390724} :answer-field/id #uuid "94a86967-5a41-5f2d-8c19-38b625daba3c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390719}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "34c426c5-7657-5020-93fd-26e20c441212" :question/math-academy-id "q-32139" :question/problem "Which of the following represents the solution to the system of linear equations given below?\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2x+6y=46 \\\\ 8x+y=-23\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "This system can be written as\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}2 & 6 \\\\ 8 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}46 \\\\ -23\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo find the solution, we need to calculate the inverse of the coefficient matrix \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 6 \\\\ 8 & 1\\end{aligned}].$\nThis gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{2\\cdot 1-6\\cdot 8}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -6 \\\\ -8 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{46}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -6 \\\\ -8 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now represent the solution to this system, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={A}^{-1}b \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{46}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -6 \\\\ -8 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}46 \\\\ -23\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302109 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115242 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "22733f71-de4a-5058-a41c-039e66e333c5" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Value of a Constant for Which a Given Linear System Is Consistent or Inconsistent"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9eefeb35-a93a-5693-8a54-a335560cde62" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300764} {:answer/id #uuid "a14a43e5-9b21-562f-aaa1-5f805da32173" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k=-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300773} {:answer/id #uuid "a2621a0b-e192-593d-b62d-8165fb69e7a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300778} {:answer/id #uuid "b3d7fb16-c926-51ed-a5b0-cbfd28c2fab5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k=-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300857} {:answer/id #uuid "f15a5a0a-88a7-5b7b-9e41-9d091c25e2a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k=4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301152}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b3d7fb16-c926-51ed-a5b0-cbfd28c2fab5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k=-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300857} :answer-field/id #uuid "0c0fdc5a-0dcc-576e-afcb-e159db8dd3fe" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301262}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "534e2a93-e71a-5475-894e-dfd5ca99a3a6" :question/math-academy-id "q-32245" :question/problem "Determine the value of $k$ for which the following system of linear equations is inconsistent:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned} & 2x-y=3 \\\\  & 4x+ky=4\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The augmented matrix for this system is\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 4 & k & 4\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nUsing the method of Gaussian elimination, we can reduce this matrix to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 4 & k & 4\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & k+2 & -2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding system is now\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned} & 2x-y=3 \\\\  & (k+2)y=-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe system is inconsistent if it has no solutions. For the system to have no solutions, one of the equations above must be false.\n\nNow if $k=-2$, then the second equation is $0=-2,$ which is false, and the system will have no solution.\n\nTherefore, our system is inconsistent when  $k=-2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115753 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115242 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "22733f71-de4a-5058-a41c-039e66e333c5" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Value of a Constant for Which a Given Linear System Is Consistent or Inconsistent"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3248af5c-24d6-51a0-a62b-8d5ab0e0e782" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300213} {:answer/id #uuid "5ed5f31b-5757-56ca-ac90-622019b8d7e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k=\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300447} {:answer/id #uuid "6b3ca3f5-57b3-5b3c-b6b4-0772e9be135c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k=\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300503} {:answer/id #uuid "6f2681ec-b8ae-51dc-9094-2e2bcadf6d8a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k=-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300522} {:answer/id #uuid "fad186f0-c1c8-5bcc-8cc3-09b1f4f1ed49" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k=-\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301203}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5ed5f31b-5757-56ca-ac90-622019b8d7e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k=\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300447} :answer-field/id #uuid "9bc72922-bebc-50a1-a8ff-483d8fbfcfc6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301729}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "2a9a3feb-6c16-50e8-885d-ee5cd242ab95" :question/math-academy-id "q-32246" :question/problem "Determine the value of $k$ for which the following system of linear equations is consistent dependent:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned} & 3x+4y=12 \\\\  & x+ky=4\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The augmented matrix for this system is\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 4 & 12 \\\\ 1 & k & 4\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nUsing the method of Gaussian elimination, we can reduce this matrix to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 4 & 12 \\\\ 1 & k & 4\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & ↔{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & k & 4 \\\\ 3 & 4 & 12\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-3){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & k & 4 \\\\ 0 & 4-3k & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding system is now \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned} & x+ky=4 \\\\  & (4-3k)y=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe system is consistent dependent if it has infinitely many solutions. For the system to have infinitely many solutions, it must have at least one free variable.\n\nIf $k=\\frac{4}{3},$ then the second equation is $0=0,$ which is true for any value of $y$, and we can remove this equation from the system. As a result, the system will contain only one equation, $x+ky=4.$ \n\nThen, the basic variable will be $x,$ and we will have a free variable $y.$ That means the system will have infinitely many solutions.\n\nTherefore, our system is consistent dependent when $k=\\frac{4}{3}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186402009 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115241 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2d81e3f1-d930-59ee-b439-2a3645aebb6c" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Consistent System of Equations Using Gauss-Jordan Elimination"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c3ccae38-2121-52d3-adbe-774101358f5f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-8-10{x}_{3} \\\\ 6+6{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{3}\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402011} {:answer/id #uuid "bd1763ae-5fcd-5d4b-b18a-af3a643749d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}8-{x}_{3} \\\\ 3+3{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{3}\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402012} {:answer/id #uuid "300bc792-b634-5ba3-8561-858fdf7d8d69" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-8-8{x}_{3} \\\\ 6-6{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{3}\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402013} {:answer/id #uuid "55911223-4948-5180-95bf-96b38f95603a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-8-6{x}_{3} \\\\ 10+6{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{3}\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402014} {:answer/id #uuid "e5a39045-c764-56cd-a094-6caa0b024ef0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-8-{x}_{3} \\\\ 6+{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{3}\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402015}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c3ccae38-2121-52d3-adbe-774101358f5f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-8-10{x}_{3} \\\\ 6+6{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{3}\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402011} :answer-field/id #uuid "94d6271a-24bd-58c0-9553-83b2dc10742e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402010}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f65d67ed-b94b-50cc-87d4-9aa7130f7547" :question/math-academy-id "q-32247" :question/problem "Consider the system of linear equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}-2{x}_{3}=4 \\\\ -2{x}_{1}-3{x}_{2}-2{x}_{3}=-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nUse the standard Gauss-Jordan method to reduce the augmented matrix $M$ of the system to reduced row echelon form, and then solve the linear system." :question/worked-solution "The augmented matrix for this system is\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & -2 & 4 \\\\ -2 & -3 & -2 & -2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nUsing the standard Gauss-Jordan method, we can reduce this matrix to reduced row echelon form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & -2 & 4 \\\\ -2 & -3 & -2 & -2\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & -2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -6 & 6\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-2){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 10 & -8 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -6 & 6\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe corresponding system is now  \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned} & {x}_{1}+10{x}_{3}=-8 \\\\  & {x}_{2}-6{x}_{3}=6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe basic variables are the variables whose coefficients are pivots. In this case, the basic variables are ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2},$ and we have one free variable, ${x}_{3}.$ That means we need to find ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ in terms of ${x}_{3}.$ \n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}=-8-10{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{2}=6+6{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}={x}_{3},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{3}$ (the free variable) is an arbitrary number.\n\nIn vector form, the solution can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-8-10{x}_{3} \\\\ 6+6{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{3}\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186390737 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115393 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d54a6dbe-42cb-5614-ae0b-611de1719b45" :knowledge-point/title "Using the Product Rules for Differentiation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e9f86cd3-7815-52c3-a642-88091b5dca5c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨20t-2,\\,36{t}^{2}+12t,\\,72{t}^{3}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390739} {:answer/id #uuid "3f10ad58-86c7-5488-87e8-2405ac572b35" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨8t-2,\\,-48{t}^{2}+12t,\\,36{t}^{3}-6⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390740} {:answer/id #uuid "b218608b-b05e-57e2-a7b7-ae74abf570fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨48t-2,\\,-362{t}^{2}+12t,\\,6⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390741} {:answer/id #uuid "a3285904-7a37-5bb0-9e4b-ea28118d456b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-28t,\\,-24{t}^{2},\\,36{t}^{3}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390742} {:answer/id #uuid "df14411c-6285-5ce6-9d91-f5503ce280c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-20t-2,\\,-72{t}^{2}+12t,\\,72{t}^{3}-6⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390743}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "df14411c-6285-5ce6-9d91-f5503ce280c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-20t-2,\\,-72{t}^{2}+12t,\\,72{t}^{3}-6⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390743} :answer-field/id #uuid "be6adfc3-be4a-5dfa-b9ef-e4320cc373e4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390738}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "5c0ab63c-e0ff-5937-9e2f-94c308127204" :question/math-academy-id "q-32347" :question/problem "Find $(f\\times g)^{′}(t)$ given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(t) & =⟨6{t}^{2},\\,2t,\\,6⟩ \\\\ g(t) & =⟨3,\\,3{t}^{2},\\,4t-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Using the cross product rule, we can write:\n\n$$\n(f\\times g)^{′}(t)={f}^{′}(t)\\times g(t)+f(t)\\times {g}^{′}(t)\n$$\n\nLet's now find the derivatives of each function.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(6{t}^{2}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(6)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨12t,\\,2,\\,0⟩ \\\\ {g}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3{t}^{2}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4t-1)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,6t,\\,4⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(t)\\times g(t) & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 12t & 2 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 3{t}^{2} & (4t-1)\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 3{t}^{2} & (4t-1)\\end{aligned}|i-|\\begin{aligned}12t & 0 \\\\ 3 & (4t-1)\\end{aligned}|j+|\\begin{aligned}12t & 2 \\\\ 3 & 3{t}^{2}\\end{aligned}|k \\\\  & =⟨8t-2,\\,12t-48{t}^{2},\\,36{t}^{3}-6⟩ \\\\ f(t)\\times {g}^{′}(t) & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 6{t}^{2} & 2t & 6 \\\\ 0 & 6t & 4\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}2t & 6 \\\\ 6t & 4\\end{aligned}|i-|\\begin{aligned}6{t}^{2} & 6 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}|j+|\\begin{aligned}6{t}^{2} & 2t \\\\ 0 & 6t\\end{aligned}|k \\\\  & =⟨-28t,\\,-24{t}^{2},\\,36{t}^{3}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(f\\times g)^{′}(t) & =⟨8t-2,\\,12t-48{t}^{2},\\,36{t}^{3}-6⟩+⟨-28t,\\,-24{t}^{2},\\,36{t}^{3}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-20t-2,\\,-72{t}^{2}+12t,\\,72{t}^{3}-6⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115764 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115393 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d54a6dbe-42cb-5614-ae0b-611de1719b45" :knowledge-point/title "Using the Product Rules for Differentiation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "875b016f-e8c7-5871-b60a-8140d518ab7e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-24t^{3} - 2t - 6 \\\\ 8 - 45t^{2} \\\\ 42t + 20\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186113551} {:answer/id #uuid "d3cb532f-811d-5a4d-a2ae-e7f634a389c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-42t - 20 \\\\ 8 - 45t^{2} \\\\ 24t^{3} + 2t + 6\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186113552} {:answer/id #uuid "14263be6-a304-5e5d-b176-0b1171e81f9e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}42t + 20 \\\\ 8 - 45t^{2} \\\\ -24t^{3} - 2t - 6\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186113553} {:answer/id #uuid "2c6e72a8-69da-5220-88ac-e36adeb439a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}8 - 45t^{2} \\\\ 42t + 20 \\\\ -24t^{3} - 2t - 6\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186113554} {:answer/id #uuid "686fc5d6-9d73-5924-be7e-292fc0f83dc3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}21t + 10 \\\\ 45t^{2} \\\\ -24t^{3} - 2t - 6\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186113555}] :answer-field/id #uuid "18574eab-4e64-5ca8-b34a-644e09dffb17" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186113550} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "564ab4d2-73e6-558b-9af0-6f5fdd10eee5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}21t+10 \\\\ 45{t}^{2} \\\\ -24{t}^{3}-2t-6\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390745} {:answer/id #uuid "08d9d97e-52f1-593e-bf6d-177ead5a4e63" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}8-45{t}^{2} \\\\ 42t+20 \\\\ -24{t}^{3}-2t-6\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390746} {:answer/id #uuid "20623cb8-9b6d-552a-a5b9-c2b26e55fa4e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-42t-20 \\\\ 8-45{t}^{2} \\\\ 24{t}^{3}+2t+6\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390747} {:answer/id #uuid "e3a88e03-753f-57ff-a632-12697f009654" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-24{t}^{3}-2t-6 \\\\ 8-45{t}^{2} \\\\ 42t+20\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390748} {:answer/id #uuid "31d0af8e-650c-555d-ad59-94751914d534" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}42t+20 \\\\ 8-45{t}^{2} \\\\ -24{t}^{3}-2t-6\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390749}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "31d0af8e-650c-555d-ad59-94751914d534" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}42t+20 \\\\ 8-45{t}^{2} \\\\ -24{t}^{3}-2t-6\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390749} :answer-field/id #uuid "e189a085-a70a-5dfd-9709-316634fc8cbc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390744}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "54437c76-a800-5517-9d6f-6a2f64f033aa" :question/math-academy-id "q-32353" :question/problem "Find $(f\\times g)^{′}(t)$ where\n\n$$\nf(t)=[\\begin{aligned}3{t}^{2} \\\\ t+4 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}],\\,g(t)=[\\begin{aligned}t+2 \\\\ -2{t}^{2} \\\\ 5t\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Using the cross product rule, we can write:\n\n$$\n(f\\times g)^{′}(t)={f}^{′}(t)\\times g(t)+f(t)\\times {g}^{′}(t)\n$$\n\nLet's now find the derivatives of each function.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3{t}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t+4) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(8)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}6t \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {g}^{′}(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t+2) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-2{t}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(5t)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -4t \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(t)\\times g(t) & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 6t & 1 & 0 \\\\ t+2 & -2{t}^{2} & 5t\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -2{t}^{2} & 5t\\end{aligned}|i-|\\begin{aligned}6t & 0 \\\\ t+2 & 5t\\end{aligned}|j+|\\begin{aligned}6t & 1 \\\\ t+2 & -2{t}^{2}\\end{aligned}|k \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}5t \\\\ -30{t}^{2} \\\\ -12{t}^{3}-t-2\\end{aligned}] \\\\ f(t)\\times {g}^{′}(t) & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 3{t}^{2} & t+4 & 8 \\\\ 1 & -4t & 5\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}t+4 & 8 \\\\ -4t & 5\\end{aligned}|i-|\\begin{aligned}3{t}^{2} & 8 \\\\ 1 & 5\\end{aligned}|j+|\\begin{aligned}3{t}^{2} & t+4 \\\\ 1 & -4t\\end{aligned}|k \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}37t+20 \\\\ 8-15{t}^{2} \\\\ -12{t}^{3}-t-4\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(f\\times g)^{′}(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}5t \\\\ -30{t}^{2} \\\\ -12{t}^{3}-t-2\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}37t+20 \\\\ 8-15{t}^{2} \\\\ -12{t}^{3}-t-4\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}42t+20 \\\\ 8-45{t}^{2} \\\\ -24{t}^{3}-2t-6\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302198 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119982 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1c8ee97e-f2b0-587e-9171-8008f1e0c4aa" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Product Rules"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2076ef48-4b79-5e2b-a49f-5b73143f1665" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2i-2j+4k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300113} {:answer/id #uuid "4898dc9d-1354-5a6b-a5ba-34d7e7f51ec9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2i-6j-4k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300329} {:answer/id #uuid "4c22e3b0-b301-5cc5-98f4-99a5c804c0fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "i+6j+2k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300359} {:answer/id #uuid "9d1ddb68-680d-5a1b-adc1-fca9bf84dcac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2i+2j-4k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300752} {:answer/id #uuid "c6e9a61d-ff93-5d17-a9dc-b27ed0977216" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4i-6j+2k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300957}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4898dc9d-1354-5a6b-a5ba-34d7e7f51ec9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2i-6j-4k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300329} :answer-field/id #uuid "af91fbde-eab9-533b-b4ed-6171c9124edd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301802}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6ba28ff2-6afb-5aba-aec7-2e0d7e59bdc3" :question/math-academy-id "q-32522" :question/problem "Find ${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}c\\times f(t)\\,\\text{d}t$ \ngiven that \n${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t=4\\,i+2\\,k$ \nand \n$c=i+j-k.$" :question/worked-solution "The cross-product rule states that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}c\\times f(t)\\,\\text{d}t=c\\times {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\n \n\nApplying the cross-product rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}c\\times f(t)\\,\\text{d}t & =c\\times {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(i+j-k)\\times (4\\,i+2\\,k) \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 4 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}|i-|\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 4 & 2\\end{aligned}|j+|\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 0\\end{aligned}|k \\\\  & =(2-0)i-(2+4)j+(0-4)k \\\\  & =2i-6j-4k.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120195 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119940 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0305491f-5ac7-5ba4-9a80-1a9a79b964f0" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating dy/dx at a Point Using dx/dy"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2104e133-914f-5ccf-9abb-bb338526ec6d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{18}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300116} {:answer/id #uuid "2e7b2fae-8ac3-57a0-bffe-05751f19e1e4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{54}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300187} {:answer/id #uuid "9e343b3d-6257-5d65-9a7e-1c0b02359997" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300760} {:answer/id #uuid "a782896a-abec-59e4-9290-6a432fe3815a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300805} {:answer/id #uuid "bebe977a-dc05-5a44-8579-fd4c6afc897a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{18}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300914}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bebe977a-dc05-5a44-8579-fd4c6afc897a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{18}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300914} :answer-field/id #uuid "38691da1-c3cd-5bcd-bc71-b36d06760bd6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301409}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e944b722-89ed-5df5-8b31-cb3570eaaf1f" :question/math-academy-id "q-32595" :question/problem "If $x=2\\sqrt{y}-\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{y}},$ find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ at the point $(5,9).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we differentiate $x(y)$ with respect to $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(2\\sqrt{y}-\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{y}}) \\\\  & =2\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(\\sqrt{y})-3\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{y}}) \\\\  & =2\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}({y}^{1/2})-3\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}({y}^{-1/2}) \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}{y}^{-1/2}-3\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{-3/2}) \\\\  & ={y}^{-1/2}+\\frac{3}{2}{y}^{-3/2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{y}}+\\frac{3}{2\\sqrt{{y}^{3}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{y}}+\\frac{3}{2y\\sqrt{y}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2y+3}{2y\\sqrt{y}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, taking the reciprocal of $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{-1}=\\frac{2y\\sqrt{y}}{2y+3}.\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting the point $(5,9),$ we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}{|}_{(x,y)=(5,9)} & =\\frac{2(9)\\sqrt{9}}{2(9)+3}=\\frac{18}{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120196 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119941 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4ff302ba-5a3f-599b-b038-7417af1dca71" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Equation of a Tangent Line Using dx/dy"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "16d73b7d-9403-55ab-b551-e19830698e26" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{x}{12}+\\frac{9}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300052} {:answer/id #uuid "6a33eeae-06c8-52d2-b05c-f45a9e998a06" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{x}{12}+\\frac{7}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300498} {:answer/id #uuid "9e0ef0fa-a60f-528b-b723-df752ee1c670" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{x}{12}+\\frac{7}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300758} {:answer/id #uuid "b2d76d1c-be51-51c6-8cde-9ba71e9a668a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=9x+3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300855} {:answer/id #uuid "b6e2c41b-b138-5e44-b020-de7bc94b2004" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=12x-27" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300874}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "16d73b7d-9403-55ab-b551-e19830698e26" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{x}{12}+\\frac{9}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300052} :answer-field/id #uuid "2a3602d1-8784-558b-8e8c-d79f5c14e514" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301360}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c7ef2446-7ddb-53cb-83cf-b4fed6b4ea6f" :question/math-academy-id "q-32596" :question/problem "Given a curve $x=\\frac{2}{3}{y}^{3}-{y}^{2},$ what is the equation of the tangent to the curve at the point $(9,3)?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we differentiate $x(y)$ with respect to $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(\\frac{2}{3}{y}^{3}-{y}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}({y}^{3})-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}({y}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot 3{y}^{2}-2y \\\\  & =2{y}^{2}-2y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, taking the reciprocal of $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{2{y}^{2}-2y}.\n$$\n\nThe slope of the tangent to the curve at the point $(9,3)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}{|}_{(x,y)=(9,3)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2(3)^{2}-2(3)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the point-slope formula for a tangent line, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-3 & =\\frac{1}{12}(x-9) \\\\ y & =\\frac{x}{12}-\\frac{3}{4}+3 \\\\ y & =\\frac{x}{12}+\\frac{9}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120198 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119978 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8c0b7d95-c699-5762-aa44-449712f035c7" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Derivative of the Inverse of a Polynomial Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "319c0fb3-aee5-5e2d-bcbf-a1949dbc941d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2{g}^{-1}(x)+6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300206} {:answer/id #uuid "896ccbe6-a82d-5b23-9d7c-b0f373d22b40" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{g}^{-1}(x)+6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300656} {:answer/id #uuid "da6d18f3-3cee-525c-86e4-e352d45bb12f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{4{g}^{-1}(x)+6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301044} {:answer/id #uuid "f2c1668d-0e47-5c9f-bcd8-84e9ad090306" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4{g}^{-1}(x)+6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301161} {:answer/id #uuid "ff0f86e0-2705-5cc9-b536-c3f797b75455" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2({g}^{-1}(x))+6{g}^{-1}(x)-2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301223}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f2c1668d-0e47-5c9f-bcd8-84e9ad090306" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4{g}^{-1}(x)+6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301161} :answer-field/id #uuid "348b05f8-ac66-5d57-a5c3-05e956512cbf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301392}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "62e6ddbb-e71d-53d4-9a83-95cdcae2804b" :question/math-academy-id "q-32629" :question/problem "If $g(x)=2{x}^{2}+6x-2,$ find ${({g}^{-1})}^{′}(x)$ as a function of ${g}^{-1}(x).$" :question/worked-solution "**Step 1:** First, we write the inverse function implicitly. The given function is $y=2{x}^{2}+6x-2,$ and to write the inverse function implicitly, we swap $x$ and $y$ as follows:\n\n$$\nx=2{y}^{2}+6y-2\n$$\n\nNote that in the equation above, we have $y={g}^{-1}(x).$\n\n**Step 2:** Next, we differentiate. The overall goal is to find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ but the equation is already solved for $x,$ so we compute $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y}$ and then take the reciprocal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(2{y}^{2}+6y-2) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =4y+6 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{4y+6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3:**\n\nLastly, we write the result in terms of ${g}^{-1}(x).$ Since $y={g}^{-1}(x),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}[{g}^{-1}(x)] & =\\frac{1}{4{g}^{-1}(x)+6} \\\\ {({g}^{-1})}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{1}{4{g}^{-1}(x)+6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302290 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119979 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0196af83-9ef6-5bab-9f50-0aacf488f0cc" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Derivative of the Inverse of a Composite Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2e68e0e6-041a-534e-ae3f-656e3055c656" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\operatorname{csc}⁡(2{g}^{-1}(x)+5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300186} {:answer/id #uuid "3d41be64-6ef0-5cb2-8c49-dfdb88df1918" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\operatorname{sec}⁡(2{g}^{-1}(x)+5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300260} {:answer/id #uuid "3ed454bc-d138-582d-9b57-ad9bfb532c15" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{csc}⁡(2{g}^{-1}(x)+5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300270} {:answer/id #uuid "77e1d8bb-f34a-54b5-88c5-760321bf0433" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2{g}^{-1}(x)+5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300552} {:answer/id #uuid "7f545b1d-f3a2-5eae-aa1d-a60253aadbbe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sec}⁡(2{g}^{-1}(x))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300594}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3d41be64-6ef0-5cb2-8c49-dfdb88df1918" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\operatorname{sec}⁡(2{g}^{-1}(x)+5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300260} :answer-field/id #uuid "f6625e80-6f3e-5f94-979a-72f6d57fbe39" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302013}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2318ec9c-3e92-5221-b4ff-d2d8a492f82d" :question/math-academy-id "q-32635" :question/problem "If $g(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x+5)-1,$ find ${({g}^{-1})}^{′}(x)$ as a function of ${g}^{-1}(x).$" :question/worked-solution "**Step 1:** First, we write the inverse function implicitly. The given function is $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x+5)-1,$ and to write the inverse function implicitly, we swap $x$ and $y$ as follows:\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2y+5)-1\n$$\n\nNote that in the equation above, we have $y={g}^{-1}(x).$\n\n**Step 2:** Next, we differentiate. The overall goal is to find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ but the equation is already solved for $x,$ so we compute $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y}$ and then take the reciprocal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2y+5)-1) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2y+5)\\cdot 2 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2y+5) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2y+5)} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sec}⁡(2y+5)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3:** Lastly, we write the result in terms of ${g}^{-1}(x).$ Since $y={g}^{-1}(x),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}[{g}^{-1}(x)] & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sec}⁡(2{g}^{-1}(x)+5) \\\\ {({g}^{-1})}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sec}⁡(2{g}^{-1}(x)+5).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186390764 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179701 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d8033e21-86ab-5dc6-8060-a0b5c134bf9d" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Regions Where a Function Is Positive or Negative Using a Sign Table"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dae056ab-7845-5fc5-8a65-cdde9b2cbb30" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "+" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390766} {:answer/id #uuid "27cd30f8-4c67-5b18-ae74-5d01e606d486" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390767}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dae056ab-7845-5fc5-8a65-cdde9b2cbb30" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "+" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390766} :answer-field/id #uuid "8a74112e-0c08-5638-a489-048657b454f6" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390765} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "452c8b52-3ea3