03cdaf3d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390776} :answer-field/id #uuid "982a0b2e-2dc8-5f75-ad31-7f4aebc78fdb" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390774}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0ff4ec42-4593-525e-b2e1-f274a6430ad8" :question/math-academy-id "q-326436" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x,y)=-{x}^{3}(y+1).$ Fill in the missing signs in the sign table of $f(x,y)$ shown below.\n\n| $y>-1$ | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $y<-1$ | {{field-3}} | {{field-4}} |\n|  | $x<0$ | $x>0$ |" :question/worked-solution "Notice that $f(x,y)$ equals zero when $x=0$ and $y=-1.$ These lines divide the $xy$-plane into four regions, as shown below.\n\n![](images/ec/ecbba4555023bdc89892cd902b30039e3a76a919b68c5a2bec670213dfc78c34.png)\n\nTo determine the sign of $f(x,y)$ in each region, we pick four test points, one for each region.\n\n![](images/9c/9ca3f95ecca25c73eadd7cbd3840cbc1f8336ff7a1a1598876c11bd4af16805b.png)\n\nNext, we calculate the sign of $f(x,y)$ in each region using a sign table:\n\n|  | Test point | $-{x}^{3}$ | $(y+1)$ | $f(x,y)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Top-Left | $(-1,0)$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| Top-Right | $(1,0)$ | $-$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n| Bottom-Left | $(-1,-2)$ | $+$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| Bottom-Right | $(1,-2)$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n\n \n\nUsing the *last* column of the table above, we get the following picture:\n\n![](images/e0/e02c333ffb3063ebf0f214cf88cb9a6d486cff0db958de6d47943f3ca0739e45.png)\n\nFinally, we fill in the required sign table.\n\n| $y>-1$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $y<-1$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n|  | $x<0$ | $x>0$ |"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302305 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119885 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e28bd3d9-649d-5cc3-a12e-e1f821caaf24" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the LU Factorization of a 3x3 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "283c298b-9742-57ea-bd3e-91b19039b7fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300155} {:answer/id #uuid "3769a266-b400-5c85-802d-d27c18680008" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300230} {:answer/id #uuid "3ceb53f1-24cc-5b40-a3c1-d1fa17f87b65" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300258} {:answer/id #uuid "96dd2d17-0520-510d-b232-b7585772c28a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300722} {:answer/id #uuid "b028918f-5597-5a96-9cfb-a914220e3b81" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300841}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b028918f-5597-5a96-9cfb-a914220e3b81" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300841} :answer-field/id #uuid "cc136613-04c4-5a55-b3e0-721f19fcb626" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301880}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "24196412-03d6-5e5f-92de-8b242613ba34" :question/math-academy-id "q-32649" :question/problem "$$\nC=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 3 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 4\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nIf $C=LU$ is the LU decomposition of the matrix $C$ given above, what is the value of ${l}_{32}\\cdot {u}_{33}?$" :question/worked-solution "Let's apply our standard LU factorization algorithm.\n\nWe reduce the matrix $C$ to row echelon form to obtain the matrix $U,$ as usual. However, instead of writing zeros below the main diagonal, we will instead write down the entries of the matrix $L.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 3 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 4\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 & -2 \\\\ \\,\\,1\\,\\, & -3 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 4\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+1{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 & -2 \\\\ \\,\\,1\\,\\, & -3 & 3 \\\\ \\,\\,0\\,\\, & -1 & 7\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\nL=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,U=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 7\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, since ${l}_{32}=-1$ and ${u}_{33}=7,$ we get\n\n$$\n{l}_{32}\\cdot {u}_{33}=(-1)\\cdot 7=-7.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120202 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119939 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "00ac6edc-908a-50be-9ef1-4b24ba597d1d" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating dy/dx Using dx/dy"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "233da062-0046-5ff6-be71-aee3cc7e71f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{y}^{3}}{4+4{y}^{4}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300130} {:answer/id #uuid "95d1ecb0-3160-5adc-97f9-910e8d9c239c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{y}^{3}}{1+{y}^{4}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300719} {:answer/id #uuid "b0a94346-e82d-5c11-b3c7-77080761ef66" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{3}}{1+{x}^{4}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300843} {:answer/id #uuid "f6dc110a-2fc4-5a9e-a3d2-5fed13a83b5e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{y}{4+4{y}^{4}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301179} {:answer/id #uuid "fae0bb00-a872-5722-aa1c-2b78582a5353" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{y}^{3}}{4+4{y}^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301204}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "233da062-0046-5ff6-be71-aee3cc7e71f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{y}^{3}}{4+4{y}^{4}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300130} :answer-field/id #uuid "84d7d2bf-ce4d-5963-b71f-6932bfd6b3ea" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301646}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "cd82fd71-e11f-52b4-879b-d6f659d732bf" :question/math-academy-id "q-32705" :question/problem "If $x=-\\frac{2}{{y}^{2}}+2{y}^{2},$ then $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=$" :question/worked-solution "First, we differentiate $x(y)$ with respect to $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(-\\frac{2}{{y}^{2}}+2{y}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(-2{y}^{-2}+2{y}^{2}) \\\\  & =-2\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}({y}^{-2})+2\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}({y}^{2}) \\\\  & =4{y}^{-3}+4y \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{{y}^{3}}+4y \\\\  & =\\frac{4+4{y}^{4}}{{y}^{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, taking the reciprocal of $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{-1}=\\frac{{y}^{3}}{4+4{y}^{4}}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302313 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119940 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0305491f-5ac7-5ba4-9a80-1a9a79b964f0" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating dy/dx at a Point Using dx/dy"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0cef3621-5613-513a-9ad2-ffc03e43c43c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "43" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300002} {:answer/id #uuid "5bfd641e-3cf0-5617-9544-a0f533f5a594" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{29}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300436} {:answer/id #uuid "985e6095-ccee-5477-87cb-a76744f1ba5b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{83}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300727} {:answer/id #uuid "ed6f600f-2b91-50be-b4b5-c60afa2e0489" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "83" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301135} {:answer/id #uuid "faa1af03-8de5-5be0-9ed5-7a51ee805bbe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{43}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301201}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "985e6095-ccee-5477-87cb-a76744f1ba5b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{83}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300727} :answer-field/id #uuid "83bae099-06f3-570f-b2ec-8dfaf4c3aae4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301643}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8588e3b0-fa4a-5436-801f-7b47fb73e332" :question/math-academy-id "q-32706" :question/problem "If $x={y}^{5}+3y-5,$ find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ at the point $(33,2).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we differentiate $x(y)$ with respect to $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}({y}^{5}+3y-5) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}({y}^{5})+3\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(y)-5\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(1) \\\\  & =5{y}^{4}+3(1)+0 \\\\  & =5{y}^{4}+3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, taking the reciprocal of $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{5{y}^{4}+3}.\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting the point $(33,2),$ we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}{|}_{(x,y)=(33,2)} & =\\frac{1}{5\\cdot {2}^{4}+3}=\\frac{1}{83}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302361 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124499 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b3699391-68e8-5c59-8ca2-83fded9f22de" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Vector Parallel To a Straight Line Given Parametrically"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1f1be446-3da9-58b3-a812-0605b95990d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 9 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300107} {:answer/id #uuid "32d9ac64-0542-5055-b1e9-6bb4066c0534" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -7 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300216} {:answer/id #uuid "bdc47ad0-9751-5257-9126-88536709155f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -9 \\\\ \\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300908} {:answer/id #uuid "d57f47fc-1f9d-59be-aebe-b95c91b0223d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301025} {:answer/id #uuid "fd00cfa2-fcdb-51f0-b98f-8000ae55ba7a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 7 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301214}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bdc47ad0-9751-5257-9126-88536709155f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -9 \\\\ \\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300908} :answer-field/id #uuid "4af4d97b-faf9-5f88-9b39-8c8fbc7ad730" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301478}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9af4fe7c-30e2-5561-acae-f8071602310e" :question/math-academy-id "q-32785" :question/problem "Consider the straight line given by the following parametric equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=3t-3 \\\\ y=7-9t \\\\ z=\\frac{3}{2}t-\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind a vector that is parallel to the line." :question/worked-solution "First, let's find the corresponding vector equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}3t-3 \\\\ 7-9t \\\\ \\frac{3}{2}t-\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 7 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}3t \\\\ -9t \\\\ \\frac{3}{2}t\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 7 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -9 \\\\ \\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ r & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 7 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -9 \\\\ \\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ \n\nThis implies that the straight line passes through the point $(-3,7,-\\frac{1}{2})$ and is parallel to the vector $v=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -9 \\\\ \\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}].$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302373 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119782 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "58148550-e41f-5df0-80dc-e783619cf87d" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Cofactor in a NxN Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1a74fc00-fd64-5b42-9d9b-22c961b3e9a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "|\\begin{aligned}7 & -3 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 8 & -2 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -9 & 4 & -1 \\\\ 5 & 2 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}|" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300080} {:answer/id #uuid "456f14d2-c05d-5f77-a95f-02402a88764b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "|\\begin{aligned}0 & 8 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 6 & 2 & -9 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 5 & 2 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 4 & 3 & 2\\end{aligned}|" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300308} {:answer/id #uuid "d80443ba-23ec-52cf-8364-fc8db3ddda93" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-|\\begin{aligned}0 & 8 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 6 & 2 & -9 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 5 & 2 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 4 & 3 & 2\\end{aligned}|" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301037} {:answer/id #uuid "e535d5d8-1a5b-50ea-bcd5-0d5de112cc6a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "|\\begin{aligned}0 & 8 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 6 & 2 & -9 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 5 & 2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 4 & 3 & 10\\end{aligned}|" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301098} {:answer/id #uuid "ea6110fe-7888-57df-94d3-e8bd05499930" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-|\\begin{aligned}0 & 8 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 6 & 2 & -9 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 5 & 2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 4 & 3 & 10\\end{aligned}|" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301122}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e535d5d8-1a5b-50ea-bcd5-0d5de112cc6a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "|\\begin{aligned}0 & 8 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 6 & 2 & -9 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 5 & 2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 4 & 3 & 10\\end{aligned}|" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301098} :answer-field/id #uuid "387a8cf8-7099-56dd-8883-b322ddd9523b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301410}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7ce9f7cf-7221-5005-8e3f-a5d9b8f1addd" :question/math-academy-id "q-32793" :question/problem "Find the cofactor of ${d}_{15}$ in \n$D=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 7 & -3 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 8 & -2 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 6 & 2 & -9 & 4 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 5 & 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 4 & 3 & 10 & 2\\end{aligned}].$" :question/worked-solution "First, to get the minor of ${d}_{15}$, we remove the $1$st row and $5$th column: \n\n$$\nD=[\\begin{aligned}∗ & ∗ & ∗ & ∗ & 2 \\\\ 0 & 8 & -2 & 0 & ∗ \\\\ 6 & 2 & -9 & 4 & ∗ \\\\ 1 & 5 & 2 & 1 & ∗ \\\\ -1 & 4 & 3 & 10 & ∗\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n \nSo, the minor is \n\n$$\n{M}_{15}=|\\begin{aligned}0 & 8 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 6 & 2 & -9 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 5 & 2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 4 & 3 & 10\\end{aligned}|.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the cofactor of ${d}_{15}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{C}_{15} & =(-1)^{1+5}{M}_{15} \\\\  & ={M}_{15} \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}0 & 8 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 6 & 2 & -9 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 5 & 2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 4 & 3 & 10\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302397 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124500 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "debd4ccc-de40-55a6-9236-9bf9fff0bdb1" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Parametric Equations of a Line"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2137033b-a3c6-5399-8de1-6c62af391ee8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}x=-1+3t \\\\ y=3+t \\\\ z=2+3t\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300121} {:answer/id #uuid "76e9f9be-afee-5c0d-937c-0f3dad104f06" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}x=3-t \\\\ y=1+3t \\\\ z=3+2t\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300549} {:answer/id #uuid "7fb1958a-34cd-52c1-9c83-11d1786cc343" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}x=-t \\\\ y=3t \\\\ z=2t\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300596} {:answer/id #uuid "c9ad2a30-8c8a-5d10-9706-7fa4ca3ab143" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}x=3t \\\\ y=t \\\\ z=3t\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300966} {:answer/id #uuid "e6bddf9a-a799-5c8b-8fce-35dc858cdbc8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}x=2-t \\\\ y=4+3t \\\\ z=5+2t\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301102}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2137033b-a3c6-5399-8de1-6c62af391ee8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}x=-1+3t \\\\ y=3+t \\\\ z=2+3t\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300121} :answer-field/id #uuid "371e7130-c050-59b2-8c27-7a7c79cf3390" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301402}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "00c48028-299a-590c-8308-e04090d60774" :question/math-academy-id "q-32817" :question/problem "![](images/1f/1ffc0945ea74b1bd467a0b9935ee28b88be004c18b11492d67ecdfec56e1bb09.png)\n\nFind the parametric equations of the line shown in the figure above." :question/worked-solution "We know that the line passes through the points $P(-1,3,2)$ and $Q(2,4,5).$ Now, we need to find a direction vector that is parallel to the given line. We can use the vector $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\overset{\\to }{PQ} \\\\  & =\\overset{\\to }{OQ}-\\overset{\\to }{OP} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the vector equation of the line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =p+tv \\\\ r & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}3t \\\\ t \\\\ 3t\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-1+3t \\\\ 3+t \\\\ 2+3t\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ And finally, the parametric equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=-1+3t \\\\ y=3+t \\\\ z=2+3t\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101380 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101023 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "41bedcde-cbc1-5634-8846-eb58ffda32e2" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Interval Given the Average Value of a Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7279328c-5005-5738-b635-464f69e0beb0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303152} {:answer/id #uuid "93b654ad-f996-520f-a174-860a20c80da1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303373} {:answer/id #uuid "d6161692-a75b-599c-a518-fe8235c34eef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303784} {:answer/id #uuid "d65358e5-c70b-5790-904f-f95c66f85282" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303789} {:answer/id #uuid "f184b873-352a-5c0e-8935-8582c50d6ad5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303968}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f184b873-352a-5c0e-8935-8582c50d6ad5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303968} :answer-field/id #uuid "e0a06460-1a11-59ee-86bb-125fd2dc2d57" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304686}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7eacf291-9083-5337-9223-5109e18f5b01" :question/math-academy-id "q-3293" :question/problem "Find the number $c>0$ such that the average value of $f(x)=9+8x-3{x}^{2}$ on the interval $[0,c]$ is equal to $-3.$" :question/worked-solution "To solve for $c,$ we apply the average value formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{\\text{avg}} & =\\frac{1}{b-a}\\,{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ -3 & =\\frac{1}{c-0}\\,{\\int }_{0}^{c}(9+8x-3{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ -3 & =\\frac{1}{c}(9x+4{x}^{2}-{x}^{3}){|}_{0}^{c} \\\\ -3 & =\\frac{1}{c}[(9c+4{c}^{2}-{c}^{3})-(0)] \\\\ -3 & =9+4c-{c}^{2} \\\\ {c}^{2}-4c-12 & =0 \\\\ (c+2)(c-6) & =0 \\\\ c & =-2,\\,6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $c>0,$ we conclude that $c=6$ only."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120217 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119829 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3f5c9c30-1745-5986-b116-e9e70a7c6161" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Vector Equation of the Plane Perpendicular to a Given Vector"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dd3ca665-8e71-5e54-9b16-e70f093f728c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r \\cdot ⟨2,-5, 3⟩ = ⟨ - 1, 2,-3⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117464} {:answer/id #uuid "fb91c921-4313-5a71-89d6-40d058571b0c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r \\cdot ⟨ - 1, 2,-3⟩ = ⟨ - 2, 5,-3⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117465} {:answer/id #uuid "1481757a-84a9-529c-9199-edf6ab25fabd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r \\cdot ⟨ - 1, 2,-3⟩ =-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117466} {:answer/id #uuid "1e422098-0791-5c2d-bf3b-caa10d3e2a60" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r \\cdot ⟨2,-5, 3⟩ = 21" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117467} {:answer/id #uuid "7503109f-7701-5a9e-a969-13e4d39ac6ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r \\cdot ⟨ - 1, 2,-3⟩ =-21" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117468}] :answer-field/id #uuid "abbeddc4-b3d9-5bc5-9a20-fe79cc8de6b3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117463} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0e448815-b2bb-5846-915b-7d9c23884bb9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r\\cdot ⟨-1,2,-3⟩=-21" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390830} {:answer/id #uuid "de65980c-0a80-5e6f-bf87-e8afefe3f527" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r\\cdot ⟨-1,2,-3⟩=⟨-2,5,-3⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390831} {:answer/id #uuid "2f57365b-0f99-52f4-983d-60b6ccab865c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r\\cdot ⟨2,-5,3⟩=⟨-1,2,-3⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390832} {:answer/id #uuid "687ca452-cc51-531c-b86f-23343784df77" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r\\cdot ⟨-1,2,-3⟩=-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390833} {:answer/id #uuid "3cdfb2c3-82ee-5d77-b2ad-4381c658ae0e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r\\cdot ⟨2,-5,3⟩=21" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390834}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0e448815-b2bb-5846-915b-7d9c23884bb9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r\\cdot ⟨-1,2,-3⟩=-21" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390830} :answer-field/id #uuid "178ed90e-697e-5682-a979-46b601de175d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390829}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "965706e8-86b7-56ea-92d3-d6b5a93e54f6" :question/math-academy-id "q-32971" :question/problem "Find an equation of the plane that contains the point $P(2,-5,3)$ and is perpendicular to the vector $n=⟨-1,2,-3⟩.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we note that the position vector of $P$ is $p=⟨2,-5,3⟩,$ and $n=⟨-1,2,-3⟩$ is normal to the plane.\n\nThe equation of the plane that passes through the point $P({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})$ and is perpendicular to the vector $n$ can be given as\n\n$$\n(r-p)\\cdot n=0,\n$$\n\nwhere $r$ is the position vector of any point on the plane, and $p$ is the position vector of $P.$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-p)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n-p\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n & =p\\cdot n \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨-1,2,-3⟩ & =⟨2,-5,3⟩\\cdot ⟨-1,2,-3⟩ \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨-1,2,-3⟩ & =-21.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304802 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119978 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8c0b7d95-c699-5762-aa44-449712f035c7" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Derivative of the Inverse of a Polynomial Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0ad77982-9d97-5298-9445-aa2f29bc75ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "({g}^{-1}(x))^{2}+12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302529} {:answer/id #uuid "3d2f1231-6fab-51a9-b30c-1f9c29aba694" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{12({g}^{-1}(x))^{2}+2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302837} {:answer/id #uuid "89a7c6b9-61e0-5fa5-b9cf-bcfa192e6661" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{12({g}^{-1}(x))^{2}+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303314} {:answer/id #uuid "97905197-8f84-5b48-bca4-efd82fb4afb0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{({g}^{-1}(x))^{2}+2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303397} {:answer/id #uuid "eba8ac9e-26d2-5904-bec3-e057833f71f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12({g}^{-1}(x))^{2}+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303931}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3d2f1231-6fab-51a9-b30c-1f9c29aba694" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{12({g}^{-1}(x))^{2}+2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302837} :answer-field/id #uuid "67848d2d-ef17-55d4-8334-d4361a471cbe" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304355}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "54994de7-137e-54bf-80bb-ec8f86707073" :question/math-academy-id "q-3300" :question/problem "Find ${({g}^{-1})}^{′}(x)$  as a function of ${g}^{-1}(x),$ if $g(x)=4{x}^{3}+2x+6.$" :question/worked-solution "**Step 1:** First, we write the inverse function implicitly. The given function is $y=4{x}^{3}+2x+6,$ and to write the inverse function implicitly, we swap $x$ and $y$ as follows:\n\n$$\nx=4{y}^{3}+2y+6\n$$\n\nNote that in the equation above, we have $y={g}^{-1}(x).$\n\n**Step 2:** Next, we differentiate. The overall goal is to find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ but the equation is already solved for $x,$ so we compute $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y}$ and then take the reciprocal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(4{y}^{3}+2y+6) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =12{y}^{2}+2 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{12{y}^{2}+2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3:** Lastly, we write the result in terms of ${g}^{-1}(x).$ Since $y={g}^{-1}(x),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}[{g}^{-1}(x)] & =\\frac{1}{12({g}^{-1}(x))^{2}+2} \\\\ {({g}^{-1})}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{1}{12({g}^{-1}(x))^{2}+2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304837 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119832 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b34272b7-a7ad-5a58-974e-54cca957d06e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Equation of the Plane Perpendicular To a Given Vector"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "144a3f0e-e55f-5127-8aba-d54af0f7928f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+y-z-18=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302589} {:answer/id #uuid "3fd6d560-4786-54eb-abee-b673f3be01d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5x-3z-13=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302857} {:answer/id #uuid "5d279caa-dcf7-5550-96a4-a74ca0a84887" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x+4y-z-13=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303024} {:answer/id #uuid "90379d74-6d2d-545b-ace3-86b0b2a1b6c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5x+y-3z-13=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303357} {:answer/id #uuid "fa35067d-2265-5860-b10f-4d3a826a567a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x+4y+z+5=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304025}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5d279caa-dcf7-5550-96a4-a74ca0a84887" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x+4y-z-13=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303024} :answer-field/id #uuid "de959850-4f0a-51e6-ac9c-e23d617a73d8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304682}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "972806e5-5276-519b-b09e-86229dab2cf0" :question/math-academy-id "q-33067" :question/problem "Find a Cartesian equation of the plane that passes through the point $P(5,0,-3)$ and is perpendicular to the vector $n=⟨2,4,-1⟩.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we note that the position vector of the point $P$ is $p=⟨5,0,-3⟩$ and the normal vector of the plane is $n=⟨2,4,-1⟩.$\n\nUsing the dot product we can find an equation of the plane as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-p)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n & =p\\cdot n \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨2,4,-1⟩ & =⟨5,0,-3⟩\\cdot ⟨2,4,-1⟩ \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨2,4,-1⟩ & =13.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to obtain the Cartesian equation of the plane, we substitute $r=⟨x,y,z⟩$ into the equation above. Hence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x,y,z⟩\\cdot ⟨2,4,-1⟩ & =13 \\\\ 2x+4y-z & =13 \\\\ 2x+4y-z-13 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304928 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119833 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2173739c-cd59-58ed-a265-8ee88c982ae0" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Equation of the Plane Perpendicular To a Given Line"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "280d2e89-c3f5-5191-859c-002f7d1d6dca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3x-5y+z+1=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302715} {:answer/id #uuid "3537ab24-c238-5848-8ae5-5ed96c1361f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3x+4z+1=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302787} {:answer/id #uuid "6d2e1a28-b548-5422-a0a4-3c14076cccdf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3x+y+4z-1=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303120} {:answer/id #uuid "9d1c65af-d3a6-51b9-87f0-3dcea485d3fc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3x-5y+2z+1=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303435} {:answer/id #uuid "f689b81d-7063-5e1e-9aaf-49529b0a36a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5x+3y+2z+1=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304004}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9d1c65af-d3a6-51b9-87f0-3dcea485d3fc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3x-5y+2z+1=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303435} :answer-field/id #uuid "b8f97666-3ba0-509b-8f76-1eba3f70428a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304577}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6438ac3c-8420-5b05-be4d-39c722849ed1" :question/math-academy-id "q-33162" :question/problem "Find a Cartesian equation of the plane that passes through the point $P(-3,0,4)$ and is perpendicular to the line \n$r=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$" :question/worked-solution "First, we note that the position vector of $P$ is $p=⟨-3,0,4⟩.$ From the vectorial equation, we find that the line is parallel to the vector $⟨3,-5,2⟩.$ So, the normal vector of the plane is \n\n$$\nn=⟨3,-5,2⟩.\n$$\n\nUsing the dot product, we can find the equation of the plane as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-p)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n & =p\\cdot n \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨3,-5,2⟩ & =⟨-3,0,4⟩\\cdot ⟨3,-5,2⟩ \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨3,-5,2⟩ & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to obtain the Cartesian equation of the plane, we substitute $r=⟨x,y,z⟩$ into the equation above. Hence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x,y,z⟩\\cdot ⟨3,-5,2⟩ & =-1 \\\\ 3x-5y+2z & =-1 \\\\ 3x-5y+2z+1 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305022 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229185 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bf16afad-30ed-5055-a86c-28affd3d8930" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Particular Solution to a First-Order Inhomogeneous ODE With Polynomial Forcing"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0cc17225-6297-5308-8f48-05ff9ca8b564" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}-x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302540} {:answer/id #uuid "3cc51a60-0768-594e-8b76-8222a817c7b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}-x-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302834} {:answer/id #uuid "4646ad58-6efb-5aab-b79b-04b4081969c8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302901} {:answer/id #uuid "548a4698-b87c-5b21-ab5a-94288a06126a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302981} {:answer/id #uuid "8cbcd5a0-a633-5eb3-bebe-8a45e59d91ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}-x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303331}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8cbcd5a0-a633-5eb3-bebe-8a45e59d91ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}-x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303331} :answer-field/id #uuid "ea696b77-54c2-547f-b092-fa65bda2a788" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304711}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "c60668bb-7444-51a7-9d21-131099a908b8" :question/math-academy-id "q-332325" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation \n\n$$\n2\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+2y={x}^{2}-2.\n$$\n\nFind the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ to this equation." :question/worked-solution "The right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $2.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $2,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha {x}^{2}+\\beta x+\\gamma \n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha ,$ $\\beta ,$ and $\\gamma $ are constants to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=2\\alpha x+\\beta .\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha ,$ $\\beta $ and $\\gamma ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n2(2\\alpha x+\\beta )+2(\\alpha {x}^{2}+\\beta x+\\gamma )={x}^{2}-2\n$$\n\nGrouping the terms on the left-hand side gives\n\n$$\n2\\alpha {x}^{2}+(4\\alpha +2\\beta )x+(2\\beta +2\\gamma )={x}^{2}-2.\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}2\\alpha =1\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,{x}^{2}) \\\\ 4\\alpha +2\\beta =0\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,x) \\\\ 2\\beta +2\\gamma =-2\\, & (\\text{equating the constants})\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =\\frac{1}{2},\\beta =-1,\\gamma =0.$\n\nTherefore, the particular integral ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}-x.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305056 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229186 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f120ae90-3df2-56d4-8a21-38c801f60d72" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the General Solution to a First-Order Inhomogeneous ODE With Polynomial Forcing"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "302ef7d0-1822-5a38-ab73-1491bcad7d49" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{-x}-{x}^{2}+4x+7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302758} {:answer/id #uuid "3cc17d40-8399-5d2d-8963-3c0c858505fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{-x}-3{x}^{2}+4x-7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302833} {:answer/id #uuid "77d72213-880b-511c-ad54-c831dbf8403d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{-x}-{x}^{2}+2x-7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303197} {:answer/id #uuid "c0976cab-e374-5089-bc45-07ea1ab7199b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{-x}+{x}^{2}-4x-7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303634} {:answer/id #uuid "dd5e0ca5-304d-5e6c-8260-7a4b0b57534a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{-x}-{x}^{2}+4x-7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303841}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dd5e0ca5-304d-5e6c-8260-7a4b0b57534a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{-x}-{x}^{2}+4x-7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303841} :answer-field/id #uuid "c502a48a-711b-525b-bb0e-6a955465c3d7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304614}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "e79c33ee-3a19-5616-a19d-f176e64aab2b" :question/math-academy-id "q-332550" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+y=-{x}^{2}+2x-3.\n$$\n\nFind the general solution to this equation given that the complementary solution is ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{-x},$ where $A$ is an arbitrary constant." :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{c}}{\\text{d}x}+{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{-x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $2.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular integral is also a polynomial of degree $2,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha {x}^{2}+\\beta x+\\gamma \n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha ,$ $\\beta $, and $\\gamma $ are constants that are to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=2\\alpha x+\\beta .\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha ,$ $\\beta ,$ and $\\gamma ,$  we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n(2\\alpha x+\\beta )+(\\alpha {x}^{2}+\\beta x+\\gamma )=-{x}^{2}+2x-3\n$$\n\nWe then group the terms on the left-hand side, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\alpha {x}^{2}+(2\\alpha +\\beta )x+(\\beta +\\gamma )=-{x}^{2}+2x-3\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}\\alpha =-1\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,{x}^{2}) \\\\ 2\\alpha +\\beta =2\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,x) \\\\ \\beta +\\gamma =-3\\, & (\\text{equating the constants})\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =-1,\\beta =4,\\gamma =-7.$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=-{x}^{2}+4x-7.\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny={y}_{c}+{y}_{p}\n$$\n\nthe general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{-x}-{x}^{2}+4x-7.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305057 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229186 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f120ae90-3df2-56d4-8a21-38c801f60d72" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the General Solution to a First-Order Inhomogeneous ODE With Polynomial Forcing"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "43c97c04-6d6c-5cee-b888-d5fea822c87c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{-x}-{x}^{2}+x+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302885} {:answer/id #uuid "6d41db8e-a0d4-5883-a31a-6aa066e3569f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{-x}-2{x}^{2}+x-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303122} {:answer/id #uuid "7b3b32e8-d05d-5cad-8868-302f72619d66" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{-x}+{x}^{2}-x-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303216} {:answer/id #uuid "b13c4481-dbc5-5e07-8c1c-ef2e613707bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{-x}-{x}^{2}-x-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303548} {:answer/id #uuid "d38a74f1-b021-5c2c-9630-9abfc4be21c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{-x}-{x}^{2}+x-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303768}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d38a74f1-b021-5c2c-9630-9abfc4be21c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{-x}-{x}^{2}+x-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303768} :answer-field/id #uuid "2b54857c-6a1b-5430-9e96-4f6e4a81a467" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304179}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "41a72880-8cb5-5bdc-96f8-c709119673ec" :question/math-academy-id "q-332552" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+y=-{x}^{2}-x-1.\n$$\n\nFind the general solution to this equation given that the complementary solution is ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{-x},$ where $A$ is an arbitrary constant." :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{c}}{\\text{d}x}+{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{-x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $2.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular integral is also a polynomial of degree $2,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha {x}^{2}+\\beta x+\\gamma \n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha ,$ $\\beta $, and $\\gamma $ are constants that are to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=2\\alpha x+\\beta .\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha ,$ $\\beta ,$ and $\\gamma ,$  we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n(2\\alpha x+\\beta )+(\\alpha {x}^{2}+\\beta x+\\gamma )=-{x}^{2}-x-1\n$$\n\nWe then group the terms on the left-hand side, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\alpha {x}^{2}+(2\\alpha +\\beta )x+(\\beta +\\gamma )=-{x}^{2}-x-1\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}\\alpha =-1\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,{x}^{2}) \\\\ 2\\alpha +\\beta =-1\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,x) \\\\ \\beta +\\gamma =-1\\, & (\\text{equating the constants})\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =-1,\\beta =1,\\gamma =-2.$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=-{x}^{2}+x-2.\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny={y}_{c}+{y}_{p}\n$$\n\nthe general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{-x}-{x}^{2}+x-2.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305076 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229184 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "77ba8502-9ddb-52d0-a885-33190dc04d4f" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Complementary Solution to a First-Order Inhomogeneous ODE"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0484600c-3d91-543d-8fcd-47f44b1e99bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "A{e}^{3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302484} {:answer/id #uuid "0cc17b3a-9bd5-59ef-a61c-7d0f59c364f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "A{e}^{-3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302541} {:answer/id #uuid "537c417b-07ad-5aee-9f67-7e20d77ca872" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "A{e}^{6x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302975} {:answer/id #uuid "754f6e90-8f3c-5a16-92d3-4f6389b2eca9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "A{e}^{-6x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303173} {:answer/id #uuid "b2e419c5-63ce-5f87-8d11-37766a45ad5a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "A\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303565}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "754f6e90-8f3c-5a16-92d3-4f6389b2eca9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "A{e}^{-6x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303173} :answer-field/id #uuid "4572d456-8fd0-5277-9867-e369d7a25850" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304268}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4d6b71e9-a6d0-5a90-9c45-dfeb0fa27c30" :question/math-academy-id "q-332642" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+6y=-5{x}^{2}+4x-7.\n$$\n\nFind the complementary solution ${y}_{c}(x)$ to this equation, where $A$ is an arbitrary constant." :question/worked-solution "To find the complementary solution, we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}^{′}+6{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nNotice that this differential equation is separable since we can write it as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{c}}{\\text{d}x}=-6{y}_{c}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we separate the variables as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{{y}_{c}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{c}}{\\text{d}x}=-6\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{{y}_{c}}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{c}}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int -6\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{{y}_{c}}\\,\\text{d}{y}_{c} & =\\int -6\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|{y}_{c}| & =-6x+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we make ${y}_{c}$ the subject by exponentiating both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|{y}_{c}| & ={e}^{-6x+C} \\\\  & ={e}^{-6x}{e}^{C} \\\\  & =K{e}^{-6x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $K={e}^{C}.$ \n\nFinally, we can drop the absolute value by allowing the arbitrary constant to be either positive or negative, i.e., setting $A=\\pm K.$  Therefore, the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{-6x},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305106 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229184 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "77ba8502-9ddb-52d0-a885-33190dc04d4f" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Complementary Solution to a First-Order Inhomogeneous ODE"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0ee9b6a9-a47e-5fbf-a0b1-2a38cc5f9e29" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "A{e}^{-10x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302553} {:answer/id #uuid "78f91522-c8b8-593f-9e70-d1b648b098cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "A{e}^{-5x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303203} {:answer/id #uuid "981be31b-8bf5-58be-900f-e1b9dd1931ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "A{e}^{5x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303401} {:answer/id #uuid "e6485508-276c-5fe2-8597-1feb6a88038e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "A\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303887} {:answer/id #uuid "ed5d07dc-a4a8-5295-80e0-3143fb2e4e5d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "A{e}^{10x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303944}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "981be31b-8bf5-58be-900f-e1b9dd1931ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "A{e}^{5x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303401} :answer-field/id #uuid "891189db-96aa-59dc-a146-acde62109448" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304442}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c4a23120-4c6b-58ce-9c4f-da56dae2e189" :question/math-academy-id "q-332727" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-5y=10.\n$$\n\nFind the complementary solution ${y}_{c}(x)$ to this equation, where $A$ is an arbitrary constant." :question/worked-solution "To find the complementary solution, we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}^{′}-5{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nNotice that this differential equation is separable since we can write it as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{c}}{\\text{d}x}=5{y}_{c}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we separate the variables as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{{y}_{c}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{c}}{\\text{d}x}=5\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{{y}_{c}}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{c}}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int 5\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{{y}_{c}}\\,\\text{d}{y}_{c} & =\\int 5\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|{y}_{c}| & =5x+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we make ${y}_{c}$ the subject by exponentiating both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|{y}_{c}| & ={e}^{5x+C} \\\\  & ={e}^{5x}{e}^{C} \\\\  & =K{e}^{5x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $K={e}^{C}.$ \n\nFinally, we can drop the absolute value by allowing the arbitrary constant to be either positive or negative, i.e., setting $A=\\pm K.$  Therefore, the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{5x},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308399 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119831 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "45cdbb0d-2986-5c05-8d23-dce29363051b" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Vector Equation of the Plane Perpendicular to a Given Straight Line"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "166ef2d2-32f9-5566-82ce-7cdba329c024" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r\\cdot ⟨-1,5,-1⟩=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305351} {:answer/id #uuid "2d1369ba-d6af-518c-9a18-2bcb2250fd78" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r\\cdot ⟨-1,5,-1⟩=-23" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305582} {:answer/id #uuid "60c376f1-f147-5aa7-8c7c-ae55262553b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r\\cdot ⟨-1,5,-1⟩=⟨1,-4,2⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306084} {:answer/id #uuid "872e10d1-c31a-5bdc-a18f-00aeaf508a92" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r\\cdot ⟨1,-4,2⟩=⟨-1,5,-1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306439} {:answer/id #uuid "d788f251-5e9e-56e8-8b45-aa84f1099d81" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r\\cdot ⟨1,-4,2⟩=-23" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307146}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2d1369ba-d6af-518c-9a18-2bcb2250fd78" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r\\cdot ⟨-1,5,-1⟩=-23" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305582} :answer-field/id #uuid "8fc16c6a-1a25-557e-b7df-b3323cb169a2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307974}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4553242a-e32b-5422-9dd6-251f47f5a320" :question/math-academy-id "q-33317" :question/problem "Find an equation of the plane that passes through the point $Q(1,-4,2)$ and is perpendicular to the line that goes through the points \n$A(2,1,-3)$ and $B(1,6,-4).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we note that the position vector of $Q$ is $q=⟨1,-4,2⟩.$\n\nSince the line  passes through the points $A(2,1,-3)$ and $B(1,6,-4)$ we can find a vector parallel to the line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =⟨1,6,-4⟩-⟨2,1,-3⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-1,5,-1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, a normal vector to the plane is $n=⟨-1,5,-1⟩.$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the plane is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-q)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n-q\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n & =q\\cdot n \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨-1,5,-1⟩ & =⟨1,-4,2⟩\\cdot ⟨-1,5,-1⟩ \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨-1,5,-1⟩ & =-23.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120248 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119830 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b0d93156-f9be-5e6e-976a-bef30dc892ee" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Vector That Is Parallel To a Plane"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3c4846e6-07fa-5199-92fa-c23138fb6c7f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305727} {:answer/id #uuid "5a1bb14c-5955-581a-b7ba-96cdefb1c303" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306012} {:answer/id #uuid "74c26a6c-96c5-502a-b012-f87b7a57e25f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306261} {:answer/id #uuid "7914febd-cdaa-5378-9536-eea1bae6ff8a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306305} {:answer/id #uuid "8c64b39c-1d55-5de4-bd2e-bb1289db7f07" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306485}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "74c26a6c-96c5-502a-b012-f87b7a57e25f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306261} :answer-field/id #uuid "b1987b64-0643-52df-ab2f-a1e652a8c54a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308086}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d32af589-36a2-5da2-a4fd-d2edd8386949" :question/math-academy-id "q-33319" :question/problem "A nonzero vector $⟨a,\\,b,\\,0⟩$ is parallel to the plane $r\\cdot ⟨6,-2,0⟩=-14.$ What is the value of $\\frac{a}{b}?$" :question/worked-solution "We need a vector $v=⟨{t}_{1},{t}_{2},{t}_{3}⟩$ that is parallel to the given plane. \n\nFrom the equation $r\\cdot ⟨6,-2,0⟩=-14$ of the plane, we get the normal vector $n=⟨6,-2,0⟩.$ So, $v⊥n$ and we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ ⟨{t}_{1},{t}_{2},{t}_{3}⟩\\cdot ⟨6,-2,0⟩ & =0 \\\\ 6{t}_{1}-2{t}_{2} & =0 \\\\ {t}_{2} & =3{t}_{1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we obtain \n\n$$\nv=⟨{t}_{1},3{t}_{1},{t}_{3}⟩,\n$$\n\nwhere ${t}_{1},{t}_{3}\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ \n\nWe are told that the third component of $v$ eqauls $0.$ So, we must set ${t}_{3}=0.$ \n\nSince $v$ is nonzero, we can pick any ${t}_{1}\\ne 0.$ For example, setting ${t}_{1}=1$, we get\n\n$$\nv=⟨1,\\,3,\\,0⟩.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\frac{a}{b}=\\frac{1}{3}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311566 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124675 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b793cda9-e11f-5a48-8053-bb75991c4c42" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Inhibited Growth Model"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0802b9a4-f869-51aa-9ad2-f5c4ec4ede2d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$19$ minutes" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308807} {:answer/id #uuid "79af7521-db1a-5890-848a-9723cc01378d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$14$ minutes" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309697} {:answer/id #uuid "851d5afb-e8fd-5832-aa19-7aad8ee508b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$12$ minutes" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309784} {:answer/id #uuid "cc811dc3-bbd1-58f9-aae7-865a2b20e03b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$10$ minutes" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310387} {:answer/id #uuid "d7357cc4-db50-5841-9068-42178a678657" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$8$ minutes" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310482}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "79af7521-db1a-5890-848a-9723cc01378d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$14$ minutes" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309697} :answer-field/id #uuid "099de7c6-8163-5944-a855-904c946df6a1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310821}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9ba6467f-d5e7-52b0-bc6e-1edae87450cd" :question/math-academy-id "q-335280" :question/problem "A can of soda is taken out of a refrigerator and left in a room that stays at a constant temperature of $20{\\,}^{∘}\\text{C}.$ The soda’s temperature $T(t)$ (in degrees Celsius) can be modeled by the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\frac{dT}{dt}=k(20-T),\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\ge 0$ is the time, measured in minutes, and $k>0$ is a constant. The initial temperature of the soda is $8{\\,}^{∘}\\text{C},$ and after $5$ minutes, the soda has warmed to $12{\\,}^{∘}\\text{C}.$ To the nearest minute, how long does it take for the soda to reach $16{\\,}^{∘}\\text{C}?$" :question/worked-solution "We're told that the temperature of the can is modeled by the inhibited growth equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dT}{dt}=k(20-T).\n$$\n\nWe separate variables and integrate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dT}{20-T} & =k\\,dt \\\\ \\int \\frac{dT}{20-T} & =\\int k\\,dt \\\\ -\\operatorname{ln}⁡|20-T| & =kt+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this is the solution to an inhibited growth equation, we have $20-T>0$ for all $t\\ge 0.$ Next, we solve for $T(t),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}20-T & ={e}^{-kt-C} \\\\ 20-T & ={e}^{-C}{e}^{-kt} \\\\ 20-T & =K{e}^{-kt} \\\\ T & =20-K{e}^{-kt}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $K={e}^{-C}$ is a constant of integration.\n\nWe're told that the initial temperature was $8{\\,}^{∘}\\text{C}.$ So, we apply the initial condition $T(0)=8,$ and solve for $K:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8 & =20-K{e}^{0} \\\\ 8 & =20-K \\\\ K & =12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to our initial value problem is\n\n$$\nT(t)=20-12{e}^{-kt}.\n$$\n\nWe're also told that, after $5$ minutes, the temperature of the soda was $12{\\,}^{∘}\\text{C}.$ So, applying the condition $T(5)=12,$  we can solve for $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12 & =20-12{e}^{-5k} \\\\ 8 & =12{e}^{-5k} \\\\ {e}^{-5k} & =\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ -5k & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{2}{3}) \\\\ k & =-\\frac{1}{5}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{2}{3})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, let ${t}_{1}$  be the time when the soda can reaches $16{\\,}^{∘}\\text{C}.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T({t}_{1}) & =16 \\\\ 20-12{e}^{-k{t}_{1}} & =16 \\\\ 4 & =12{e}^{-k{t}_{1}} \\\\ {e}^{-k{t}_{1}} & =\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ -k{t}_{1} & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{3}) \\\\ {t}_{1} & =-\\frac{1}{k}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{3})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the value of $k,$ we get:\n\n$$\n{t}_{1}=\\frac{-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{3})}{-\\frac{1}{5}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{2}{3})}=13.547…≈14\\text{ }\\text{minutes},\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest minute."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311567 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124675 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b793cda9-e11f-5a48-8053-bb75991c4c42" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Inhibited Growth Model"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "156ded23-ce88-53d3-a639-9d820eb09a01" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$1$ minute" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308909} {:answer/id #uuid "8a644610-216c-5c70-96be-6978f05c7dd1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$3$ minutes" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309835} {:answer/id #uuid "9a4012d6-aa43-57f5-b933-67f5ae4bf20a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$4$ minutes" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309990} {:answer/id #uuid "c0fcc147-8682-560f-8b96-51d7e1858066" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$2$ minutes" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310305} {:answer/id #uuid "f721e0cb-70f5-51e1-acaf-e8fb8aacbddd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$5$ minutes" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310714}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "156ded23-ce88-53d3-a639-9d820eb09a01" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$1$ minute" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308909} :answer-field/id #uuid "47f6fe49-3d76-5e25-8d26-c4ee0c65a685" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310985}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "645d4a44-d927-5a86-b205-e23eb598edfc" :question/math-academy-id "q-335282" :question/problem "The volume $X(t)$ (in $\\text{L}$) of water in a container as it fills toward its maximum capacity can be modeled by the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dX}{dt}=0.4(3-X),\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\ge 0$ is the time, measured in minutes. If the initial volume is $1.5\\,\\text{L},$ how long does it take for the container to reach a volume of $2\\,\\text{L}?$ Round your answer to the nearest integer." :question/worked-solution "We're told that the container's volume is modeled by the inhibited growth equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dX}{dt}=0.4(3-X).\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate our differential equation by separating the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dX}{3-X} & =0.4\\,dt \\\\ \\int \\frac{dX}{3-X} & =\\int 0.4\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ -\\operatorname{ln}⁡|3-X| & =0.4t+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this is the solution to an inhibited growth equation, we have $3-X>0$ for all $t\\ge 0.$\nNext, we solve for $X(t),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3-X & ={e}^{-0.4t-C} \\\\ 3-X & ={e}^{-C}{e}^{-0.4t} \\\\ 3-X & =K{e}^{-0.4t} \\\\ X & =3-K{e}^{-0.4t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $K={e}^{-C}$ is a constant of integration.\n\nWe're told that the initial volume was $1.5\\,\\text{L}.$ So, we apply the initial condition $X(0)=1.5,$ and solve for $K:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1.5 & =3-K{e}^{0} \\\\ 1.5 & =3-K \\\\ K & =1.5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to our initial value problem is\n\n$$\nX(t)=3-1.5{e}^{-0.4t}.\n$$\n\nFinally, let ${t}_{1}$ be the time when the volume is $2\\,\\text{L}.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X({t}_{1}) & =2 \\\\ 3-1.5{e}^{-0.4{t}_{1}} & =2 \\\\ 1 & =1.5{e}^{-0.4{t}_{1}} \\\\ {e}^{-0.4{t}_{1}} & =\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ -0.4{t}_{1} & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{2}{3}) \\\\ {t}_{1} & =-\\frac{1}{0.4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{2}{3}) \\\\  & =1.013… \\\\  & ≈1\\,\\text{minute},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest integer."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186391617 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119737 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3ff1822c-afad-54f8-aa36-7e0de79d9d04" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Unit Tangent Vector at a Given Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9f60c71f-f1a0-5dc9-bc85-bc53e3b5d9e8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}\\,i+\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{6}}\\,j+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391619} {:answer/id #uuid "2f82adaf-7d79-546e-b51a-3d2064321d15" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}\\,i+\\frac{2}{3}\\,j+\\frac{1}{3}\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391620} {:answer/id #uuid "10b01e1d-27d8-5784-b829-e7428d99b479" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\,i+\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391621} {:answer/id #uuid "8b39b63d-e746-538f-8858-f3d5381d09d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{6}}\\,i+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}\\,j+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391622} {:answer/id #uuid "6560a5a6-5578-53c6-881e-58e2deb05371" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\,i-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391623}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2f82adaf-7d79-546e-b51a-3d2064321d15" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}\\,i+\\frac{2}{3}\\,j+\\frac{1}{3}\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391620} :answer-field/id #uuid "3d43ad92-b445-5432-aa1f-111fa6dcfbd4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391618}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "93a1c03f-77b3-5fa3-b1a1-22418386cc31" :question/math-academy-id "q-33584" :question/problem "What is the unit tangent vector to the curve $r(t)=2t\\,i+{t}^{2}\\,j+t\\,k$ at the point $P(2,1,1)?$" :question/worked-solution "For a curve $r(t)=x(t)\\,j+y(t)\\,j+z(t)\\,k$, the unit tangent vector $T(t)$ at the point $P(x(t),y(t),z(t))$ is given by \n\n$$\nT(t)=\\frac{{r}^{′}(t)}{‖{r}^{′}(t)‖}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the value of $t$ that corresponds to the point $P(2,1,1)$ on the given curve. We know that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =2\\,i+\\,j+\\,k \\\\ 2t\\,i+{t}^{2}\\,j+t\\,k & =2\\,i+\\,j+\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the respective components gives the system\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}2t=2 \\\\ {t}^{2}=1 \\\\ t=1.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the first equality, $t=1.$ This value satisfies the other two equations. Therefore, the point $P(2,1,1)$ is at the tip of $r(1).$ \n\nNow, we calculate ${r}^{′}(t)$ and evaluate it at $t=1:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2})\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)\\,k \\\\  & =2\\,i+2t\\,j+\\,k \\\\ {r}^{′}(1) & =2\\,i+2(1)\\,j+\\,k \\\\  & =2\\,i+2\\,j+\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the magnitude of ${r}^{′}(1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{r}^{′}(1)‖ & =\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{2}^{2}+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9} \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we normalize ${r}^{′}(1)$ and obtain the unit tangent vector to the given curve at $P(2,1,1):$  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(1) & =\\frac{{r}^{′}(1)}{‖{r}^{′}(1)‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(2\\,i+2\\,j+\\,k) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}\\,i+\\frac{2}{3}\\,j+\\frac{1}{3}\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311665 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119833 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2173739c-cd59-58ed-a265-8ee88c982ae0" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Equation of the Plane Perpendicular To a Given Line"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6b698b8d-d838-569e-8743-c7334a7dd440" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4y+2z-4=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309583} {:answer/id #uuid "a0f0c8b9-bc37-5bfb-9929-0df4d8029937" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4y+z-3=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310049} {:answer/id #uuid "a57fb5b0-6222-58fe-bfc1-5e5325cd7d81" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4y+z-4=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310083} {:answer/id #uuid "b5a86eb1-28e1-5734-84d2-b468a6108b74" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+4y+z-4=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310216} {:answer/id #uuid "cfa6a16f-d3a1-5a8e-b635-4f53092ff5f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4y-z-4=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310419}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a57fb5b0-6222-58fe-bfc1-5e5325cd7d81" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4y+z-4=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310083} :answer-field/id #uuid "c0d80fa9-92b4-56ea-bacd-670e1a568b19" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311349}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "69ff4417-28a7-50ac-aae7-8483ea57f4a8" :question/math-academy-id "q-336213" :question/problem "Find a Cartesian equation of the plane that passes through the point $P(2,1,0)$ and is perpendicular to the line \n${\\begin{aligned}x=1 \\\\ y=-2+4t \\\\ z=3+t\\end{aligned}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we note that the position vector of $P$ is $p=⟨2,1,0⟩.$ From the parametrical equation, we find that the line is parallel to the vector $⟨0,4,1⟩.$ So, the normal vector of the plane is \n\n$$\nn=⟨0,4,1⟩.\n$$\n\nUsing the dot product, we can find the equation of the plane as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-p)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n & =p\\cdot n \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨0,4,1⟩ & =⟨2,1,0⟩\\cdot ⟨0,4,1⟩ \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨0,4,1⟩ & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to obtain the Cartesian equation of the plane, we substitute $r=⟨x,y,z⟩$ into the equation above. Hence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x,y,z⟩\\cdot ⟨0,4,1⟩ & =4 \\\\ 4y+z & =4 \\\\ 4y+z-4 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363934 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119833 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2173739c-cd59-58ed-a265-8ee88c982ae0" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Equation of the Plane Perpendicular To a Given Line"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "de24ce31-8567-5933-91b9-84ce46195f82" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5y-4z+2=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363936} {:answer/id #uuid "f47bdf6d-aa44-5993-8a4f-e55f9cddaa83" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y-4z+2=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363937} {:answer/id #uuid "a3a0927d-77b7-5bf0-bc22-7b9ddcfd7658" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5y-4z-22=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363938} {:answer/id #uuid "9c6c0173-9824-5097-808b-03437fc112f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5x-4z+2=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363939} {:answer/id #uuid "c1a6b20d-9141-5a21-8147-f66bb2f7a01f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5x-4y-22=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363940}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "de24ce31-8567-5933-91b9-84ce46195f82" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5y-4z+2=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363936} :answer-field/id #uuid "c325860e-c4c3-52ab-a5ca-c8d3a61bbc17" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363935}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2b22ad79-7dea-54b4-b8ec-aee9009a09ff" :question/math-academy-id "q-336218" :question/problem "Find a Cartesian equation of the plane that passes through the point $P(0,2,3)$ and is perpendicular to the line \n\n$$\n\\frac{y-3}{-5}=\\frac{z}{4},\\,x=1\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we note that the position vector of $P$ is $p=⟨0,2,3⟩.$ From the Cartesian equation, we find that the line is parallel to the vector $⟨0,-5,4⟩.$ So, the normal vector of the plane is \n\n$$\nn=⟨0,-5,4⟩.\n$$\n\nUsing the dot product, we can find the equation of the plane as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-p)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n & =p\\cdot n \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨0,-5,4⟩ & =⟨0,2,3⟩\\cdot ⟨0,-5,4⟩ \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨0,-5,4⟩ & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to obtain the Cartesian equation of the plane, we substitute $r=⟨x,y,z⟩$ into the equation above and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x,y,z⟩\\cdot ⟨0,-5,4⟩ & =2 \\\\ -5y+4z & =2 \\\\ -5y+4z-2 & =0 \\\\ 5y-4z+2 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311722 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119737 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3ff1822c-afad-54f8-aa36-7e0de79d9d04" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Unit Tangent Vector at a Given Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1f1d1fdd-a6f6-5a38-a3df-d7b2a2583f2d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨\\frac{2}{3},\\,\\frac{1}{3},\\,0⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308987} {:answer/id #uuid "3345984a-6431-5bc2-8a0b-3b698dfa4caf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{6}},\\,\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}},\\,\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309139} {:answer/id #uuid "5fe0a0a5-7a27-538d-a1c1-12e36b2b78a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨\\frac{1}{3},\\,\\frac{2}{3},\\,\\frac{2}{3}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309470} {:answer/id #uuid "74254c1b-ae29-507e-9365-aa3345a018e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨\\frac{2}{3},\\,\\frac{1}{3},\\,\\frac{2}{3}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309649} {:answer/id #uuid "77f37899-ebfb-5a89-8ed1-b0406f53dd75" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨\\frac{2}{3},\\,\\frac{2}{3},\\,\\frac{1}{3}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309690}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "74254c1b-ae29-507e-9365-aa3345a018e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨\\frac{2}{3},\\,\\frac{1}{3},\\,\\frac{2}{3}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309649} :answer-field/id #uuid "9756b53b-26a7-53d0-942d-24a3a520cea9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311234}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e09c97e7-cdca-5648-a0b0-ca6d528dfc46" :question/math-academy-id "q-33655" :question/problem "What is the unit tangent vector to the curve $r(t)=⟨{t}^{4},\\,{t}^{2},\\,4t⟩$ at the point $P(1,1,4)?$" :question/worked-solution "For a curve $r(t)=⟨x(t),y(t),z(t)⟩$, the unit tangent vector $T(t)$ at the point $P(x(t),y(t),z(t))$ is given by\n\n$$\nT(t)=\\frac{{r}^{′}(t)}{‖{r}^{′}(t)‖}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the value of $t$ that corresponds to the point $P(1,1,4)$ on the given curve. We know that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =⟨1,\\,1,\\,4⟩ \\\\ ⟨{t}^{4},\\,{t}^{2},\\,4t⟩ & =⟨1,\\,1,\\,4⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the respective components gives the system\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}{t}^{4}=1 \\\\ {t}^{2}=1 \\\\ 4t=4.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the third equality, we get $t=1.$ This value satisfies the other two equations. Therefore, the point $P(1,1,4)$ is at the tip of $r(1).$  \n\nNow, we calculate ${r}^{′}(t)$ and evaluate it at $t=1:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{4}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨4{t}^{3},\\,2t,\\,4⟩ \\\\ {r}^{′}(1) & =⟨4(1)^{3},\\,2(1),\\,4⟩ \\\\  & =⟨4,\\,2,\\,4⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we calculate the magnitude of ${r}^{′}(1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{r}^{′}(1)‖ & =\\sqrt{{4}^{2}+{2}^{2}+{4}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{36} \\\\  & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we normalize ${r}^{′}(1)$ and obtain the unit tangent vector to the given curve at $P(1,1,4):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(1) & =\\frac{{r}^{′}(1)}{‖{r}^{′}(1)‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}⟨4,\\,2,\\,4⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{2}{3},\\,\\frac{1}{3},\\,\\frac{2}{3}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120269 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119737 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3ff1822c-afad-54f8-aa36-7e0de79d9d04" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Unit Tangent Vector at a Given Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a9e8358a-0fe4-5a96-bc54-ab8f95b3b9cc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨\\frac{2}{9}, \\frac{8}{9}, \\frac{4}{9}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117754} {:answer/id #uuid "a9f48b93-11de-5e78-98e8-b65cc354a8fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{69}}, \\frac{8}{\\sqrt{69}}, \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{69}}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117755} {:answer/id #uuid "df886ea5-f00c-5ff5-a834-74a737b97eb0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{17}}, 0, \\frac{4}{\\sqrt{17}}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117756} {:answer/id #uuid "263e9f7f-77be-56c6-be00-ec3dc9df9110" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨\\frac{1}{9},-\\frac{8}{9}, \\frac{4}{9}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117757} {:answer/id #uuid "53f5109b-ddff-55d1-8efe-8a3d183bd239" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨\\frac{1}{9}, \\frac{8}{9}, \\frac{4}{9}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117758}] :answer-field/id #uuid "9cf60ba8-539d-53fb-8665-0db326cff953" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117753} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "101c1f8f-90f7-5afb-9a2a-9d9d07341ab6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{17}},0,\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{17}}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365787} {:answer/id #uuid "e7153d39-ff14-5c43-a90f-c9463613ccd2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨\\frac{2}{9},\\frac{8}{9},\\frac{4}{9}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365788} {:answer/id #uuid "7b4893f4-d976-5d25-b643-929e8ba9edcb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{69}},\\frac{8}{\\sqrt{69}},\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{69}}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365789} {:answer/id #uuid "d9f9c136-39ba-5393-9857-3e448e41c70a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨\\frac{1}{9},\\frac{8}{9},\\frac{4}{9}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365790} {:answer/id #uuid "ca08ec85-b4ca-5869-bc18-73f064ee828a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨\\frac{1}{9},-\\frac{8}{9},\\frac{4}{9}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365791}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d9f9c136-39ba-5393-9857-3e448e41c70a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨\\frac{1}{9},\\frac{8}{9},\\frac{4}{9}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365790} :answer-field/id #uuid "25954b1d-fcf3-5456-bffb-5aa7bddaf2a7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365786}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7fefbcf2-d508-588d-ab4f-814e78edeb11" :question/math-academy-id "q-33662" :question/problem "What is the unit tangent vector to the curve $r(t)=⟨t+1,\\,8\\operatorname{ln}⁡t,\\,2{t}^{2}+3⟩$ at the point $P(2,0,5)?$" :question/worked-solution "For a curve $r(t)=⟨x(t),y(t),z(t)⟩$, the unit tangent vector $T(t)$ at the point $P(x(t),y(t),z(t))$ is given by\n\n$$\nT(t)=\\frac{{r}^{′}(t)}{‖{r}^{′}(t)‖}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the value of $t$ that corresponds to the point $P(2,0,5)$ on the given curve. We know that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =⟨2,0,5⟩ \\\\ ⟨t+1,\\,8\\operatorname{ln}⁡t,\\,2{t}^{2}+3⟩ & =⟨2,0,5⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the respective components gives the system\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}t+1=2 \\\\ 8\\operatorname{ln}⁡t=0 \\\\ 2{t}^{2}+3=5.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the first equality, $t=1.$ This value also satisfies the other two equations. Therefore, $P(2,0,5)$ is at the tip of $r(1).$ \n\nNow, we calculate ${r}^{′}(t)$ and evaluate it at $t=1:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t+1),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(8\\operatorname{ln}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2{t}^{2}+3)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,\\frac{8}{t},\\,4t⟩ \\\\ {r}^{′}(1) & =⟨1,\\,\\frac{8}{1},\\,4(1)⟩=⟨1,8,4⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we calculate the magnitude of ${r}^{′}(1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{r}^{′}(1)‖ & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{8}^{2}+{4}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+64+16} \\\\  & =9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we normalize ${r}^{′}(1)$ and obtain the unit tangent vector to the given curve at $P(2,0,5):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(1) & =\\frac{{r}^{′}(1)}{‖{r}^{′}(1)‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}⟨1,8,4⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{1}{9},\\frac{8}{9},\\frac{4}{9}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311862 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119966 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "55abc561-2d1a-5da7-80f0-f52ea8c1bc3b" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Orthogonal Trajectories of a One-Parameter Family"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2bd435dd-0d23-523a-b22c-021099293641" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "xy=K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309078} {:answer/id #uuid "5d113c1b-eeac-582b-adfc-27b973e4b4d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=Kx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309446} {:answer/id #uuid "625ce389-3726-5627-b3ee-ab0bc649071e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=Ky" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309495} {:answer/id #uuid "74ff317f-1066-599e-939c-5940ef563f60" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}={x}^{2}+K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309659} {:answer/id #uuid "bb7f960d-6940-5e33-a0f0-24a8fdfaa2f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310268}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "74ff317f-1066-599e-939c-5940ef563f60" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}={x}^{2}+K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309659} :answer-field/id #uuid "6ec673ff-c866-5730-b940-d8c2bc43cd6e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311086}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "85065fe3-60b8-561d-a678-99a217a32ec3" :question/math-academy-id "q-337570" :question/problem "Find the orthogonal trajectories of the family of curves $xy=C,$ where $C$ and $K$ are parameters." :question/worked-solution "Orthogonal trajectories are families of curves that intersect another given family of curves at right angles. In other words, the tangent lines of curves from each family are perpendicular at every intersection point.\n\nLet's determine the slope of the family of curves given by $xy=C.$ First, we implicitly differentiate with respect to $x$ and solve for the derivative of $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{d}{dx}(xy) & =\\frac{d}{dx}(C) \\\\ y+x{y}^{′} & =0 \\\\ x{y}^{′} & =-y \\\\ {y}^{′} & =-\\frac{y}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that the parameter $C$ has already been eliminated.\n\nFor a curve to intersect this family orthogonally, its slope ${y}_{⊥}^{′}$ must be the negative reciprocal of the slope ${y}^{′}$ above. Hence,\n\n$$\n{y}_{⊥}^{′}=-\\frac{1}{{y}^{′}}=-\\frac{1}{(-\\frac{y}{x})}=\\frac{x}{y}.\n$$\n\nHence, the orthogonal trajectories must satisfy\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{x}{y}.\n$$\n\nTo find the general solution, we separate the variables and integrate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y\\cdot \\frac{dy}{dx} & =x \\\\ \\int y\\cdot \\frac{dy}{dx}\\,dx & =\\int x\\,dx \\\\ \\int y\\,dy & =\\int x\\,dx \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{2} & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+{K}_{1} \\\\ {y}^{2} & ={x}^{2}+K\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $K=2{K}_{1}$ is a constant of integration.\n\nTherefore, the orthogonal trajectories of the family of curves $xy=C,$ where $C$ is a parameter, are given by\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}={x}^{2}+K,\n$$\n\nwhere $K$ is a parameter. Some curves from each family for specific parameter values are given in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/98/98159dfb60fd280468ad8165475b5ba58e41658174a932b4c9555ef1153a577c.png)\n\n**Note**: For the slopes ${y}^{′}$ and ${y}_{⊥}^{′},$ we divide by $x$ and $y,$ respectively. So, the orthogonal trajectories are only defined on the region $x,y\\ne 0.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120275 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119860 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1889ba1a-09d4-5021-9ed2-3e531f8d5ef0" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Arc Length of a Three-Dimensional Spatial Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5e49651a-bdf3-5daa-a8ac-b0f9a1acf337" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309453} {:answer/id #uuid "683d57e8-3476-5871-b8ce-02f1e4add2e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309550} {:answer/id #uuid "8f378059-5350-57fd-9b5f-1f9ef63dc60c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309892} {:answer/id #uuid "b4bd39a7-2f29-5a63-9c46-e2b53b0a1e5a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310211} {:answer/id #uuid "d51c476d-8e1e-5c39-9e95-2b6de637ee4f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310465}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b4bd39a7-2f29-5a63-9c46-e2b53b0a1e5a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310211} :answer-field/id #uuid "d3ec7fac-5ac8-5f09-95e8-e286bea551a9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311408}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d7f25b66-94f4-5ecd-88ef-6b78d26ced87" :question/math-academy-id "q-33779" :question/problem "Find the arc length of the curve $f(t)=⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}{t}^{2}⟩$\nfrom $t=1$ to $t=3.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find ${f}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{f}^{′}(t)‖:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}{t}^{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t),\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t),\\,\\sqrt{3}t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,\\sqrt{3}t⟩ \\\\ ‖{f}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{(t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}+(t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}+(\\sqrt{3}t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{t}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t)+3{t}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4{t}^{2}} \\\\  & =2t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $t>0$ for $t\\in [1,3].$\n\nTherefore, the arc length of the curve is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}‖{f}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}2t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={t}^{2}{|}_{1}^{3} \\\\  & =9-1 \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311887 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078588 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ec3823db-6df8-502a-a050-b5aa169d2e9d" :knowledge-point/title "Applying Differential Operators With Constant Coefficients"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0ece56a7-2b9e-5963-82f3-afd35b2ef55f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-14\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)+6\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308852} {:answer/id #uuid "18bbe9be-2d41-5c1c-b79c-cf4637c7fbb7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-14\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)-6\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308946} {:answer/id #uuid "827f3d8f-9357-5033-a261-d291e7358e23" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)+6\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309771} {:answer/id #uuid "b33023be-155f-5252-ad31-05ef1a857408" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310197} {:answer/id #uuid "e53ddad0-75e6-5572-8437-d745ea97b6ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-9\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)+6\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310576}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0ece56a7-2b9e-5963-82f3-afd35b2ef55f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-14\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)+6\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308852} :answer-field/id #uuid "c8bb3f36-623f-5108-95b5-f9fd39c91460" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311370}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "89358ed4-7921-5b21-8db0-91c53ef07d47" :question/math-academy-id "q-337831" :question/problem "Given that $D=\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+2\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}-5,$ what is $D(y)$ for $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)?$" :question/worked-solution "The given operator is a second-order differential operator of the form\n\n$$\nD(y)={a}_{2}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+{a}_{1}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+{a}_{0}y\n$$\n\nwith constant coefficients  ${a}_{2}=1,{a}_{1}=2,$ and ${a}_{0}=-5.$ \n\nTo compute $D(y),$ we apply the definition of the operator $D$ to the function $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D(y) & =D(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)) \\\\  & =(\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+2\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}-5)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x) \\\\  & =\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x))+2\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x))-5\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x))+2\\cdot (3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x))-5\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x) \\\\  & =-9\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)+6\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)-5\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x) \\\\  & =-14\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)+6\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311895 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078590 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7066d712-f0a3-554f-9d10-3d6fe4b95a18" :knowledge-point/title "Applying Differential Operators With Multivariable Functions as Coefficients"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2b8f3513-5b8a-5990-a2ea-afe65e000a3e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "({x}^{2}+1)(7-{x}^{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309074} {:answer/id #uuid "613e7864-c0ff-5dc1-8f19-729eb88b3bb5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "({x}^{2}+1)(2{x}^{2}-7)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309488} {:answer/id #uuid "65703eea-b67d-5bae-a3d2-77443977fc00" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "({x}^{2}+1)(7-2{x}^{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309530} {:answer/id #uuid "7572e9bd-6d21-5462-802d-d0c4f6676ba4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "({x}^{2}+1)(7+2{x}^{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309663} {:answer/id #uuid "a0620ca7-2629-5016-866e-6f57277aee4a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "({x}^{2}+1)(5-2{x}^{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310046}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "65703eea-b67d-5bae-a3d2-77443977fc00" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "({x}^{2}+1)(7-2{x}^{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309530} :answer-field/id #uuid "63bdc93a-a7dc-5f29-8234-fea73d37241e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311056}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "49f57d8f-907e-5009-afba-de523ee59540" :question/math-academy-id "q-337848" :question/problem "If $D(y)=y\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}-xy\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+5y,$ then $D({x}^{2}+1)=$" :question/worked-solution "The given operator is a second-order differential operator of the form\n\n$$\nD(y)={f}_{2}(x,y)\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+{f}_{1}(x,y)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+{f}_{0}(x,y)y\n$$\n\nwith ${f}_{2}=y,{f}_{1}=-xy,$ and ${f}_{0}=5.$\n\nTo compute $D(y),$ we apply the definition of the operator $D$ to the function $y={x}^{2}+1:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D({x}^{2}+1) & =({x}^{2}+1)\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}({x}^{2}+1)-x({x}^{2}+1)\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}+1)+5({x}^{2}+1) \\\\  & =({x}^{2}+1)\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2x)-x({x}^{2}+1)\\cdot 2x+5({x}^{2}+1) \\\\  & =({x}^{2}+1)\\cdot 2-2{x}^{2}({x}^{2}+1)+5({x}^{2}+1) \\\\  & =2({x}^{2}+1)-2{x}^{2}({x}^{2}+1)+5({x}^{2}+1) \\\\  & =7({x}^{2}+1)-2{x}^{2}({x}^{2}+1) \\\\  & =({x}^{2}+1)(7-2{x}^{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186397097 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152274 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "90930e15-0827-5e0b-82cf-98263fc756d6" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Second-Order Homogeneous ODEs"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8b5ec8b6-d55b-55cc-aa94-056dd87936ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "homogeneous" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397099} {:answer/id #uuid "d8f3b959-aec4-56a5-ac52-93392b485d16" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "inhomogeneous" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397100}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8b5ec8b6-d55b-55cc-aa94-056dd87936ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "homogeneous" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397099} :answer-field/id #uuid "01b3194e-1c5b-5f63-8af7-cad12a568ef1" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397098} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "01e0647b-2813-5eba-b947-eeac3bfb20f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "homogeneous" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397102} {:answer/id #uuid "f34ae965-4585-556a-b037-c112e1a82720" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "inhomogeneous" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397103}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f34ae965-4585-556a-b037-c112e1a82720" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "inhomogeneous" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397103} :answer-field/id #uuid "2ac21c56-a1ed-58af-8f2a-f8f3a643de10" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397101} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fdd2df26-875b-5cf8-8e35-d22dc66dd099" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "homogeneous" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397105} {:answer/id #uuid "61df961a-7458-5bdd-bf21-6f1ffc7be0e8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "inhomogeneous" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397106}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "61df961a-7458-5bdd-bf21-6f1ffc7be0e8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "inhomogeneous" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397106} :answer-field/id #uuid "f8930119-dde1-5c7f-9dc6-b7a8c29faee5" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397104}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8da06f22-6a38-578e-b590-3e2d4a9025f6" :question/math-academy-id "q-338004" :question/problem "Determine which of the following second-order ODEs are homogeneous, and which are inhomogeneous.$\\,$ The equation $\\,\\sqrt{x}\\,{y}^{″}-2x{y}^{′}+y=0$ is {{field-1}}. \n\n$\\,$ The equation $\\,{y}^{″}+3{y}^{′}=x$ is {{field-2}}. \n\n$\\,$ The equation $\\,{y}^{″}-xy+4x=0$ is {{field-3}}." :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have a second-order linear ODE given in the form\n\n$$\na(x){y}^{″}+b(x){y}^{′}+c(x)y=f(x).\n$$\n\nThe equation is homogeneous if $f(x)=0$ for all $x.$ If it is not homogeneous, then it is inhomogeneous.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each option.\n\n- The equation\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{x}\\,{y}^{″}-2x{y}^{′}+y=0\n$$\n\nis $\\text{homogeneous}$ since, for the right-hand side, we have $f(x)=0$ for all $x.$\n\n- The equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+3{y}^{′}=x\n$$\n\nis $\\text{inhomogeneous}$ since, for the right-hand side, we have $f(x)=x,$ which is different from zero.\n\n- Writing the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}-xy+4x=0\n$$\n\nin the form $a{y}^{″}+b{y}^{′}+cy=f,$ we have\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}-xy=-4x.\n$$\n\nThis equation is $\\text{inhomogeneous}$ since, for the right-hand side, we have $f(x)=-4x,$ which is different from zero."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186397107 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152274 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "90930e15-0827-5e0b-82cf-98263fc756d6" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Second-Order Homogeneous ODEs"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8224d08c-b6d5-59c1-a3e9-c4005d2ce278" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "homogeneous" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397109} {:answer/id #uuid "51641394-cf83-58e5-aa34-474bd8353286" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "inhomogeneous" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397110}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "51641394-cf83-58e5-aa34-474bd8353286" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "inhomogeneous" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397110} :answer-field/id #uuid "84f1c5b4-e827-513e-b3a4-9b5e916f4d90" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397108} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c982b65b-e870-508e-bf5e-ddcdb473e18a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "homogeneous" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397112} {:answer/id #uuid "7497ecc8-2fd5-55ef-ad5f-c1e4eba3b91f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "inhomogeneous" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397113}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7497ecc8-2fd5-55ef-ad5f-c1e4eba3b91f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "inhomogeneous" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397113} :answer-field/id #uuid "cbe235fa-bcf8-5953-9422-9db01e330fa5" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397111} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c21f26fa-303e-5e74-bcc7-27f756e59fef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "homogeneous" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397115} {:answer/id #uuid "81be49a9-a2c9-51ce-9466-8f23516f81e9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "inhomogeneous" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397116}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c21f26fa-303e-5e74-bcc7-27f756e59fef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "homogeneous" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397115} :answer-field/id #uuid "ee6a8920-f14a-5940-b560-371ca67535b3" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397114}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e8b12875-ae8a-5464-b1d4-8be3ec87c5f9" :question/math-academy-id "q-338082" :question/problem "Determine which of the following second-order ODEs are homogeneous, and which are inhomogeneous.$\\,$ The equation $\\,{y}^{″}+2y=x$ is {{field-1}}. \n\n$\\,$ The equation $\\,\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x){y}^{″}+{y}^{′}-6y=0$ is {{field-2}}. \n\n$\\,$ The equation $\\,{y}^{″}-(x+2)y+5x=0$ is {{field-3}}." :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have a second-order linear ODE given in the form\n\n$$\na(x){y}^{″}+b(x){y}^{′}+c(x)y=f(x).\n$$\n\nThe equation is homogeneous if $f(x)=0$ for all $x.$ If it is not homogeneous, then it is inhomogeneous.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each option.\n\n- The equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+2y=x\n$$\n\nis $\\text{inhomogeneous}$ since, for the right-hand side, we have $f(x)=x,$ which is different from zero.\n\n- The equation\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x){y}^{″}+{y}^{′}-6y=0\n$$\n\nis $\\text{homogeneous}$ since, for the right-hand side, we have $f(x)=0$ for all $x.$\n\n- Writing the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}-(x+2)y+5x=0\n$$\n\nin the form $a{y}^{″}+b{y}^{′}+cy=f,$ we have\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}-(x+2)y=-5x.\n$$\n\nThis equation is $\\text{inhomogeneous}$ since, for the right-hand side, we have $f(x)=-5x,$ which is different from zero."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311921 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119934 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "62196ae9-df2e-5a3a-bc0a-1c0f4c40af3e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Normal Vector To a Plane Given by Parametric Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3999a87b-f882-5ca7-9260-d448846a37d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-3,5,4⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309187} {:answer/id #uuid "39a5a717-471c-5a17-834f-af512678a057" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-13,33,-14⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309188} {:answer/id #uuid "7600e36d-9294-520d-95e5-38e4fd4ac988" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-12,21,-4⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309669} {:answer/id #uuid "9bc1f13f-1145-50d7-80ea-c9565ed86989" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-33,13,-41⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310008} {:answer/id #uuid "fe53bf4d-a431-5742-8703-83bd73a22ce3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨4,7,-1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310782}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9bc1f13f-1145-50d7-80ea-c9565ed86989" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-33,13,-41⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310008} :answer-field/id #uuid "86549ea0-3e13-5e9e-9d1d-fbd94e79de20" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311169}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8c5d0e74-0671-5cb7-b3b8-23383905b037" :question/math-academy-id "q-33819" :question/problem "Find a normal vector to the plane\n\n$$\nr=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 23 \\\\ 14\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]+s[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 7 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],\\,t,\\,s\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$" :question/worked-solution "From the parametric equation, we have that $v=⟨-3,5,4⟩$ and $w=⟨4,7,-1⟩$ are parallel to the plane.\n\nNow, we need to find a normal vector of the plane. To do that, we use the cross product of $v$ and $w$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =v\\times w \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]\\times [\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 7 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ -3 & 5 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 7 & -1\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =-33i+13j-41k \\\\  & =⟨-33,13,-41⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, a normal vector to the plane is $n=⟨-33,13,-41⟩.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311926 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078590 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7066d712-f0a3-554f-9d10-3d6fe4b95a18" :knowledge-point/title "Applying Differential Operators With Multivariable Functions as Coefficients"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "449a764c-8f81-5c3b-9b6e-e0a26299202d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x(8x\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x-3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309272} {:answer/id #uuid "7df9a7a2-56d2-5626-95a8-b0a042202120" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x(3+8x\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309732} {:answer/id #uuid "a05e0d04-4ef4-513b-809d-b3ff81ce1d4e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x(6-8x\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310045} {:answer/id #uuid "af22f291-1db4-5cee-a064-896df1e2431f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310165} {:answer/id #uuid "bfb09209-2ab4-5dc8-b332-7c852960e719" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x(3-8x\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310295}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bfb09209-2ab4-5dc8-b332-7c852960e719" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x(3-8x\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310295} :answer-field/id #uuid "8a0ffc14-7fa4-51a7-a7e6-2da759ff8baa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311185}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "cefcc57d-275c-5178-8314-12212c2c75ed" :question/math-academy-id "q-338254" :question/problem "If $D(y)=xy\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{3}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{3}}+3y,$ then $D(\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x)=$" :question/worked-solution "The given operator is a third-order differential operator of the form\n\n$$\nD(y)={f}_{3}(x,y)\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{3}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{3}}+{f}_{2}(x,y)\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+{f}_{1}(x,y)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+{f}_{0}(x,y)y\n$$\n\nwith ${f}_{3}=xy,{f}_{2}=0,{f}_{1}=0,$ and ${f}_{0}=3.$\n\nTo compute $D(y),$ we apply the definition of the operator $D$ to the function $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D(\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x) & =x\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{3}}{\\text{d}{x}^{3}}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x)+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x \\\\  & =x\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x)+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x \\\\  & =x\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x)+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x \\\\  & =x\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x\\cdot (-8\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x)+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x(3-8x\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x)\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186402242 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152276 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2d0a7ede-b896-56c6-9b60-a6aec8eff9e8" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Second-Order Initial Value Problems"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "aababf37-9c33-5d0f-9433-1d0cf555f97b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402244} {:answer/id #uuid "3fa28dc5-5323-59c1-89c5-ec291f51cc7d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402245} {:answer/id #uuid "fe799d91-4567-55b5-9d6d-065742893e23" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402246} {:answer/id #uuid "0560bd9f-1114-574e-9bb5-08d3e454f4bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402247} {:answer/id #uuid "f1484f26-148e-5676-bd10-764ffcb28ef8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402248}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3fa28dc5-5323-59c1-89c5-ec291f51cc7d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402245} :answer-field/id #uuid "de781819-e735-5446-8359-21f70d676ce4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402243}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c104c7c7-be1b-5f7c-9495-548d3d354240" :question/math-academy-id "q-339251" :question/problem "Which of the following systems is an initial value problem?\n\nI. ${x}^{2}{y}^{″}-2{y}^{′}+y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x,\\,y(4)=1,\\,{y}^{′}(4)=-1$\nII. $x{y}^{″}+{y}^{′}-2y=9$\nIII. ${x}^{2}{y}^{″}-8x{y}^{′}+16y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,\\,y(2)=0,\\,{y}^{′}(2)=3$" :question/worked-solution "If a second-order differential equation for the function $y(x)$ is given with two conditions specified at the *same* location in the domain of $y,$ then this is an **initial value problem**.\n\nLet's go through each equation in turn.\n\n- The first system is an initial value problem. The differential equation is together with two conditions at the same location in the domain of $y(x)$ (i.e., at $x=4$).\n\n- The second system is not an initial value problem. It contains an equation only. No conditions are specified.\n\n- The third system is an initial value problem. The differential equation is together with two conditions at the same location in the domain of $y(x)$ (i.e., at $x=2$)."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186402249 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152276 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2d0a7ede-b896-56c6-9b60-a6aec8eff9e8" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Second-Order Initial Value Problems"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8892f2f5-e370-5028-8460-a0e49e8ec9cc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402251} {:answer/id #uuid "88eda162-b9e7-53c5-aaf0-8ab22fd5c91a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402252} {:answer/id #uuid "42ef59eb-cad5-5519-b0d9-e972100a8fd9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402253} {:answer/id #uuid "eddbd1d4-bb8e-5d05-a1f6-e513d4b1a8cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402254} {:answer/id #uuid "e7ad3aa9-ab9b-50bd-8028-70bf56ae664a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402255}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "42ef59eb-cad5-5519-b0d9-e972100a8fd9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402253} :answer-field/id #uuid "5e74f1dd-50b5-55df-99cd-c5f3fc2522c6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402250}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e7b3292e-ae09-5cdf-bbb6-9d5f24a711b9" :question/math-academy-id "q-339256" :question/problem "Which of the following systems is an initial value problem?\n\nI. $x{y}^{″}-{y}^{′}+2y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,\\,y(0)=1,\\,{y}^{′}(0)=-1$\nII. ${x}^{2}{y}^{″}+{y}^{′}-y=5x$\nIII. ${x}^{2}{y}^{″}-3x{y}^{′}+4y={x}^{2},\\,y(1)=0,\\,{y}^{′}(4)=2$" :question/worked-solution "If a second-order differential equation for the function $y(x)$ is given with two conditions specified at the *same* location in the domain of $y,$ then this is an **initial value problem**.\n\nLet's go through each equation in turn.\n\n- The first system is an initial value problem. The differential equation is together with two conditions at the same location in the domain of $y(x)$ (i.e., at $x=0$).\n\n- The second system is not an initial value problem. It contains an equation only. No conditions are specified.\n\n- The third system is not an initial value problem. It consists of a differential equation together with two conditions at different points in the domain of $y(x)$ (i.e., at $x=1$ and $x=4$). This is a **boundary value problem.**"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314815 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147494 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "680a42fd-9b70-5fb2-916c-d10b71b11d82" :knowledge-point/title "Classifying Equilibrium Points Using Phase Lines"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0da9d30b-9af5-5213-8917-9446376514c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312071} {:answer/id #uuid "589393df-7bc7-5a90-8263-53d67c64983c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312685} {:answer/id #uuid "70f839cb-952f-531a-b542-262e1a7103b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312863} {:answer/id #uuid "78cc97f9-33e7-59d4-9b18-c4c9a83c9ded" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312937} {:answer/id #uuid "ebe8553b-5c76-5132-885a-602051f2f1e4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313858}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "78cc97f9-33e7-59d4-9b18-c4c9a83c9ded" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312937} :answer-field/id #uuid "13b7a5d2-c30b-5f43-a916-f63fc3d12cae" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314063}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b869e45a-6b1f-53e1-aacc-10ffe2baaefd" :question/math-academy-id "q-339664" :question/problem "![](images/52/528bf1bc9d6177f031b8b5504641903a11dfc2c2576e4759bf4cb7c278d0585c.png)\n\nA particular autonomous differential equation has the phase line shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $y=0$ is a sink\n- $y=3$ is a source\n- $y=3$ is a node" :question/worked-solution "We can classify the equilibrium solutions of an autonomous ODE ${y}^{′}=f(y)$ by examining its phase line.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *sink* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *toward* the equilibrium solution.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *source* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *away* from the equilibrium solution.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *node* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line point in the same direction.\n\nLet's check each statement:\n\n- Statement I is true. Notice that the arrow just above the point $y=0$ points downward, and the arrow just below it points upward. In other words, both arrows point *toward* the point $y=0.$ This means that $y=0$ is a sink.\n\n- Statement II is true, while statement III is false. Notice that the arrow just above the point $y=3$ points upward, and the arrow just below it points downward. In other words, both arrows point *away* from $y=3.$ This means that $y=3$ is a source.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314828 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068735 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b428331b-4916-5b2c-8aa6-89a2358cdef0" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Indefinite Integral of a Exponential-Trigonometric Expression Using Integration by Parts"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "386e98c1-73bb-5454-b219-d1a0b2cbcd4f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{2x}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x))}{13}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312432} {:answer/id #uuid "8d06227a-dba4-573b-8ee8-7fe7ab605c64" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{2x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x))}{13}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313101} {:answer/id #uuid "8f8ccfeb-d107-5fd5-a633-7ce78226c89b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)-{e}^{2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)-{e}^{2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)}{13}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313120} {:answer/id #uuid "eba8d785-f5ae-5b9c-bb6e-c13c6ab5bfa7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)-2{e}^{2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)-3{e}^{2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)}{13}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313855} {:answer/id #uuid "f49e2812-02a9-51d6-8932-6f7fc008518b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)+{e}^{2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)-{e}^{2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)}{13}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313932}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "386e98c1-73bb-5454-b219-d1a0b2cbcd4f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{2x}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x))}{13}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312432} :answer-field/id #uuid "87e94abc-25eb-5e8f-9907-5fdc535a7393" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314371}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "84d99744-2a44-5b9d-88cd-43a5f0c291b3" :question/math-academy-id "q-3397" :question/problem "$\\int {e}^{2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x=$" :question/worked-solution "To use integration by parts, let's suppose that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={e}^{2x}\\, & ⇒\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=2{e}^{2x}\\,, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\, & ⇒\\,v=\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x=-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =\\int {e}^{2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={e}^{2x}(-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)}{3})-\\int (-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)}{3})(2{e}^{2x})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{{e}^{2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)}{3}+\\frac{2}{3}\\int {e}^{2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{{e}^{2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)}{3}+\\frac{2}{3}J.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAgain, we can use integration by parts to evaluate\n\n$$\nJ=\\int {e}^{2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTo do this, let's suppose that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={e}^{2x}\\, & ⇒\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=2{e}^{2x}\\,, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\, & ⇒\\,v=\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}J & =\\int {e}^{2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={e}^{2x}(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)}{3})-\\int (\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)}{3})(2{e}^{2x})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)}{3}-\\frac{2}{3}\\int {e}^{2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)}{3}-\\frac{2}{3}I.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting this back into our equation for $I,$ we can subsequently solve for $I.$ We remember to add a constant of integration $C$ at the end.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =-\\frac{{e}^{2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)}{3}+\\frac{2}{3}J \\\\ I & =-\\frac{{e}^{2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)}{3}+\\frac{2}{3}(\\frac{{e}^{2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)}{3}-\\frac{2}{3}I) \\\\ I & =-\\frac{{e}^{2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)}{3}+\\frac{2{e}^{2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)}{9}-\\frac{4}{9}I \\\\ \\frac{13}{9}I & =\\frac{2{e}^{2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)-3{e}^{2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)}{9} \\\\ I & =\\frac{{e}^{2x}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x))}{13}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314936 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147496 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b114a373-697f-5005-bb1b-46202547a905" :knowledge-point/title "Classifying the Equilibrium Points of a Given Differential Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2664b839-fbf0-567b-afa3-646482fdf79b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$y=-4$ is a sink, and $y=3$ is a source" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312267} {:answer/id #uuid "32813065-d3d6-58b0-9d4a-bdd1cb2f9aaa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$y=-4$ and $y=3$ are both sinks" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312383} {:answer/id #uuid "a307810d-8144-56de-bb87-5ddcac01d99a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$y=-4$ is a source, and $y=3$ is a sink" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313257} {:answer/id #uuid "d0967e73-2a1d-5e59-865b-9dd854354884" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$y=-4$ is a sink, and $y=3$ is a node" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313606} {:answer/id #uuid "e4fd4f50-6174-522a-9273-6e274ff5689d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$y=-4$ and $y=3$ are both nodes" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313785}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2664b839-fbf0-567b-afa3-646482fdf79b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$y=-4$ is a sink, and $y=3$ is a source" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312267} :answer-field/id #uuid "253807bb-639e-5c30-ab4c-1f98e708236c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314107}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "57368a00-fbb0-5aa3-a452-73c74c695262" :question/math-academy-id "q-340352" :question/problem "Which of the following statements is true regarding the equilibrium solutions of ${y}^{′}={y}^{2}+y-12?$" :question/worked-solution "We can classify the equilibrium solutions of an autonomous ODE ${y}^{′}=f(y)$ by examining its phase line.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *sink* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *toward* the equilibrium solution.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *source* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *away* from the equilibrium solution.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *node* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line point in the same direction.\n\nFirst, we need to find the equilibrium solutions. So, we solve $f(y)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(y) & =0 \\\\ {y}^{2}+y-12 & =0 \\\\ (y+4)(y-3) & =0 \\\\ y & =-4,3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the equilibrium solutions are $y=-4$ and $y=3.$ \n\nNow, we analyze the sign of $f(y)$ using a sign table, as follows:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty ,-4)$ | $(-4,3)$ | $(3,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y+4$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $y-3$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n| $f(y)$ | $+$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n\nSo, we have that $f(y)$ is positive for $y\\in (-\\infty ,-4)\\cup (3,\\infty ),$ and negative for $y\\in (-4,3).$\n\nWe can now draw the phase line for this equation.\n\n![](images/c2/c2bb2cbc11afb9bb2b25fb2105d19c2748541592c77bcecab342db69834b4fe2.png)\n\nFinally, we classify the equilibrium solutions:\n\n- $y=-4$ is a sink because the nearby solutions converge toward this point.\n\n- $y=3$ is a source because the nearby solutions diverge away from this point.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$y=-4$ is a sink, and $y=3$ is a source\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314972 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119808 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "aaefbc53-9df9-54a0-a969-66e062553d5c" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Vector Lies in the Span of Given Vectors"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "077cc4da-3c12-5749-87e3-9136fa56ff18" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312025} {:answer/id #uuid "39d686df-f396-5c82-92b8-3b075c274d61" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312445} {:answer/id #uuid "4dd5ec6b-997e-5c4c-82cd-f74e85dcbdf1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312607} {:answer/id #uuid "7578cf04-a90c-5b5d-ac09-511fd3e57a8d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312909} {:answer/id #uuid "eced6632-dc68-59a9-a39d-d5b16448d6d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "b\\notin \\text{Span}{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313868}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7578cf04-a90c-5b5d-ac09-511fd3e57a8d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312909} :answer-field/id #uuid "901fd7a3-f614-5740-8413-9326f82935f3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314396}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "dffa9b37-7313-5190-ae5f-372cf9f5aaea" :question/math-academy-id "q-34058" :question/problem "The vectors ${a}_{1}$, ${a}_{2}$, and $b$ are given below. Determine if $b\\in \\text{Span}{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}.$ In the affirmative case, find the value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}$ from the decomposition $b={x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}.$\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We know that $b\\in \\text{Span}{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}$ if there exist ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ such that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2} & =b \\\\ {x}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}=1 \\\\ {x}_{2}=1 \\\\ 2{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2}=7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve the system above, we set up and row reduce an augmented matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & [\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 3 & 7\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}-2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 5\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}-5{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have the system\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}=1 \\\\ {x}_{2}=1 \\\\ 0=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation, we get ${x}_{2}=1.$ Substituting into the first equation gives \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}-1=1\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=2.\n$$\n\nSince the system has a solution, we can conclude that $b\\in \\text{Span}{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}.$\n\nMoreover, there is exactly one solution, so we have exactly one way to write $b$ as a linear combination of ${a}_{1}$ and ${a}_{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{a}_{1}+{a}_{2} & =b \\\\ 2\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=2\\cdot 1=2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186315094 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119923 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5130f54d-2a53-5373-81a6-355921ea7523" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Vector That Makes a Set Linearly Dependent"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0016720f-74fa-55b6-bf01-800b13e638e0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 10\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311959} {:answer/id #uuid "08ce3a9c-f71f-58ed-81a6-9432cacf1320" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -10\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312039} {:answer/id #uuid "2edaefbb-f2cd-577d-8e0d-d90240242ca9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 15\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312347} {:answer/id #uuid "62232f19-6365-5d74-863b-0168911e0afa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ -15\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312758} {:answer/id #uuid "efe860a9-b664-5498-b242-e6bf0e7b70af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 15\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313897}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "efe860a9-b664-5498-b242-e6bf0e7b70af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 15\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313897} :answer-field/id #uuid "7b0d7e63-bf87-56e8-ad2d-ee39fe6ec062" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314337}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5d50385e-e4f4-5265-bb61-4639aa5b495f" :question/math-academy-id "q-34142" :question/problem "The set ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}$ is linearly dependent. Which of the following vectors could be ${a}_{2}$ if\n${a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}]?$" :question/worked-solution "We say that two vectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ are *linearly independent* if the only solution to the equation\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{v}_{1}+{x}_{2}{v}_{2}=0\n$$\n\nis ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}=0.$ Otherwise, the vectors are *linearly dependent*.\n\nA set containing *only two* vectors is linearly *dependent* if and only if the two vectors are parallel.\n\nFrom the given options, the correct answer is \n\n$$\n{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 15\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo see why, notice that \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 15\\end{aligned}]=-3[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}]=-3{a}_{1}.\n$$\n\nThis way, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{a}_{1}-3{a}_{1} & =0 \\\\ 3{a}_{1}+{a}_{2} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn other words, the vectors ${a}_{1}$ and ${a}_{2}$ are parallel.\n\nTo see why this means that ${a}_{1}$ and ${a}_{2}$ are linearly dependent, we notice that the equation\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}=0\n$$\n\nhas the solution ${x}_{1}=3$ and ${x}_{2}=1,$ which is different from ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}=0.$ Therefore, the set is linearly dependent."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120300 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119925 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "52a45814-d1e2-5948-b7bd-69dcaf6cd0eb" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Linearly Independent and Dependent Sets Using Row Reduction"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "822ddbd5-58de-5fea-9cf7-1fcbe86f67cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117940} {:answer/id #uuid "96af24ee-f190-5ae5-95e4-2c700e3c3503" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117941} {:answer/id #uuid "e9c6879f-2d58-53e5-896a-a25733cccd9b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117942} {:answer/id #uuid "1c219b15-3bf1-5781-9424-d826c1ed20af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117943} {:answer/id #uuid "34cec551-a885-5fdc-95a2-f60a69416744" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117944}] :answer-field/id #uuid "7f729d47-2792-5b7b-b557-b37c1a7f41ec" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117939} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4162d50c-81cd-5f40-945e-55e7fbb90d71" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364079} {:answer/id #uuid "5c442bb0-6c4a-5605-97d8-7c10a412af08" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364080} {:answer/id #uuid "46cc0c5f-07f0-54ed-a508-6823ad071f1e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364081} {:answer/id #uuid "aeb77505-a33c-51be-8117-b6192c0d360c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364082} {:answer/id #uuid "641b4be1-7f54-5a77-b6cb-f23af77a0838" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364083}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "641b4be1-7f54-5a77-b6cb-f23af77a0838" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364083} :answer-field/id #uuid "c92c8641-7770-5cbc-adff-e1f92e5b0f87" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364078}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "dd1b519a-6daa-58c6-a554-f53dcb225d1e" :question/math-academy-id "q-34153" :question/problem "Consider the following vectors:\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n \n\nAssume that ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},$ and ${x}_{3}$ are real numbers. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The equation ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}=0$ has only the trivial solution ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}=0.$\n- The vectors ${a}_{1},{a}_{2},$ and ${a}_{3}$ are linearly dependent.\n- The vectors ${a}_{1},{a}_{2},$ and ${a}_{3}$ are linearly independent." :question/worked-solution "We say that three vectors ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},$ and ${v}_{3}$ are *linearly independent* if the only solution to the equation\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{v}_{1}+{x}_{2}{v}_{2}+{x}_{3}{v}_{3}=0\n$$\n\nis ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}=0.$ Otherwise, the vectors are *linearly dependent*.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\nThe equation ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}=0$ corresponds to the system\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-2{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ {x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+2{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ 3{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve the system, we create its augmented matrix and reduce it using Gaussian elimination:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-1){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the matrix above, we conclude that the basic variables are ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$, while ${x}_{3}$ is the free variable. \n\nSince there is one free variable, the equation ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}=0$ has an infinite number of nonzero solutions, which means that the vectors are linearly dependent. \n\nTherefore, statements I and III are false, while statement II is true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317213 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119808 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "aaefbc53-9df9-54a0-a969-66e062553d5c" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Vector Lies in the Span of Given Vectors"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1d12c5f6-3b92-5441-a0fc-3952c1a70749" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315285} {:answer/id #uuid "2be071fa-e50f-5e15-af2e-800d12bec8db" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315374} {:answer/id #uuid "40c87c4f-e4ad-5573-b56b-bbf8371657d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315491} {:answer/id #uuid "719e49b9-6453-5c6f-ae8b-fb5f2080b954" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315762} {:answer/id #uuid "a449e927-6b10-5f40-a19c-fccc1dc264eb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "b\\notin \\text{Span}{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316048}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "40c87c4f-e4ad-5573-b56b-bbf8371657d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315491} :answer-field/id #uuid "8429c92b-0897-5b2d-8856-66b5640c1f28" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316855}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "54dedc31-eea3-5db1-a0e4-025cee199163" :question/math-academy-id "q-34218" :question/problem "The vectors ${a}_{1}$, ${a}_{2}$, ${a}_{3}$, and $b$ are given below. Determine if $b\\in \\text{Span}{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}}.$ In the affirmative case, find the value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}\\cdot {x}_{3}$ from the decomposition $b={x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}.$\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ -14 \\\\ -14\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We know that $b\\in \\text{Span}{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}}$ if there exist ${x}_{1}$, ${x}_{2}$ and ${x}_{3}$ such that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3} & =b \\\\ {x}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{3}[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ -14 \\\\ -14\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-2{x}_{3}=6 \\\\ -3{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+6{x}_{3}=-14 \\\\ -3{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}=-14\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve the system above, we set up and row reduce an augmented matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & [\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -2 & 6 \\\\ -3 & 1 & 6 & -14 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 1 & -14\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -2 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 1 & -14\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+3{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -2 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & -2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have the system\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-2{x}_{3}=6 \\\\ {x}_{2}=4 \\\\ {x}_{3}=-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the third equation, we get ${x}_{3}=-2.$ Substituting into the first equation gives \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}-2(-2)=6\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=2.\n$$\n\nSince the system has a solution, we can conclude that $b\\in \\text{Span}{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}}.$\n\nMoreover, there is exactly one solution, so we have exactly one way to write $b$ as a linear combination of ${a}_{1}$, ${a}_{2}$ and ${a}_{3}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{a}_{1}+4{a}_{2}+(-2){a}_{3} & =b \\\\ 2\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+4\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]+(-2)\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ -14 \\\\ -14\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}\\cdot {x}_{3}=2\\cdot 4\\cdot (-2)=-16.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186391790 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119806 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "590953b8-2533-5a4b-bf4f-f002b0367919" :knowledge-point/title "Solving for a Vector in a Linear Combination"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3a7c078d-19ef-546c-aac2-8965df8b5900" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "13" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391792} {:answer/id #uuid "17dfda34-666b-58e7-95c0-b80675d1c00f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391793} {:answer/id #uuid "58d99fc6-7b1d-5ec4-8b66-4dc4564dfcbb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391794} {:answer/id #uuid "95f4185d-53f9-500f-aa63-d2fdf30556e9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391795} {:answer/id #uuid "0e5469f2-4e80-52c6-9765-adf91fc0519f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391796}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3a7c078d-19ef-546c-aac2-8965df8b5900" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "13" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391792} :answer-field/id #uuid "d42b67bc-bf40-52c3-9b93-8c498d0d9ad8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391791}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6ca48b5a-ff7c-5739-a4dc-d774d869fa76" :question/math-academy-id "q-34247" :question/problem "Find the value of ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}$ given that $3a+2x=b$ and\n\n$$\na=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{4}\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}24 \\\\ 8 \\\\ -15 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Using the properties of addition/subtraction and scalar multiplication of vectors to isolate $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3a+2x & =b \\\\ 2x & =b-3a \\\\ x & =\\frac{1}{2}(b-3a) \\\\ x & =\\frac{1}{2}([\\begin{aligned}24 \\\\ 8 \\\\ -15 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]-3[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]) \\\\ x & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}12 \\\\ 14 \\\\ -12 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\ x & =[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 7 \\\\ -6 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=6+7=13.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317320 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119925 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "52a45814-d1e2-5948-b7bd-69dcaf6cd0eb" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Linearly Independent and Dependent Sets Using Row Reduction"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "24069f64-c580-5200-b5e0-1c7ef2ae7e68" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315321} {:answer/id #uuid "3a15928d-e29a-514a-aa9c-b81e928ccc22" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315451} {:answer/id #uuid "6b92d802-3085-528a-9c72-efc57cf5c2c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315724} {:answer/id #uuid "800f5135-a705-5bc5-b182-cd0428d00e1a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315821} {:answer/id #uuid "93c3185c-8c40-5110-addf-07d04037df9e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315941}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3a15928d-e29a-514a-aa9c-b81e928ccc22" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315451} :answer-field/id #uuid "22bb0e27-d6d9-5966-9fb5-317681ddbc7c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316627}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1120ac93-1146-5212-b323-41cae9ac4014" :question/math-academy-id "q-34276" :question/problem "Consider the following vectors:\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nAssume that ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},$ and ${x}_{3}$ are real numbers. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The equation ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}=0$ has only the trivial solution ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}=0.$\n- The vectors ${a}_{1},{a}_{2},$ and ${a}_{3}$ are linearly independent.\n- The vectors ${a}_{1},{a}_{2},$ and ${a}_{3}$ are linearly dependent." :question/worked-solution "We say that three vectors ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},$ and ${v}_{3}$ are *linearly independent* if the only solution to the equation\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{v}_{1}+{x}_{2}{v}_{2}+{x}_{3}{v}_{3}=0\n$$\n\nis ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}=0.$ Otherwise, the vectors are *linearly dependent*.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\nThe equation ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}=0$ corresponds to the system\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-2{x}_{2}+3{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ {x}_{2}-{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ -3{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}-4{x}_{3}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve the system, we create its augmented matrix and reduce it using Gaussian elimination:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1 & -4 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -5 & 5 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+5{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the matrix above, we conclude that the basic variables are ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$, while ${x}_{3}$ is the free variable. \n\nSince there is one free variable, the equation ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}=0$ has an infinite number of nonzero solutions, which means that the vectors are linearly dependent. \n\nTherefore, statements I and II are false, while statement III is true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060270 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059685 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "993c8fe4-dae9-5dc4-9f94-5012f905e0ee" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Correct Quadrant for Negative Angles that Contain More Than One Full Rotation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "252d775f-77ed-511b-95ad-524d7bb4cd39" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "IV" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315326} {:answer/id #uuid "6bcab351-5c37-5d1b-99d8-985db29eacd6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315726} {:answer/id #uuid "969cb243-da0c-5f4c-8e30-aedc2baa4e6e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Between quadrants III and IV" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315954} {:answer/id #uuid "ae942c7b-d556-576d-99b4-c2d5e0d5ad8c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316110} {:answer/id #uuid "e7b0d548-2819-5f8e-b792-8ccfa0fb789c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316400}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "969cb243-da0c-5f4c-8e30-aedc2baa4e6e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Between quadrants III and IV" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315954} :answer-field/id #uuid "e94ae561-6bac-5708-8ab7-f7ca8354b9fd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317100}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8483a20c-9e8e-5588-94ee-dd0e0014806e" :question/math-academy-id "q-34325" :question/problem "The angle $\\theta =-{450}^{∘}$ is represented in the coordinate plane in the usual way. In which quadrant does it lie?" :question/worked-solution "First, we find an angle that's coterminal with $-{450}^{∘}$ by *adding* integer multiples of ${360}^{∘}$ until we get an angle that lies in the range $[{0}^{∘},{360}^{∘}).$\n\n- $-{450}^{∘}+{360}^{∘}=-90$\n\n- $-{450}^{∘}+2\\cdot {360}^{∘}={270}^{∘}\\,✓$\n\nSo the angle ${270}^{∘}$ is coterminal with $-{450}^{∘}.$ Let's draw this coterminal angle in the coordinate plane.\n\n![](images/35/35b9783c9ac87b0a8d177e7bdf340b48513146404b7c768a8532da59b6ec7948.png)\n\nWe see that ${270}^{∘}$ lies between quadrants III and IV. Therefore, $-{450}^{∘}$ also lies between these quadrants."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186385276 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186217246 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "734fe467-57d6-5b3f-a61e-1e2e4624b5fc" :knowledge-point/title "Using Abel's Identity to Conclude Independence/Dependence"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "db2971e6-1fc2-5a15-b561-63195b136074" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385278} {:answer/id #uuid "6b0fa27a-ed94-5c90-a481-896ad99baa7c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{Q(x)}{{x}^{2}+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385279} {:answer/id #uuid "7c03e432-04c5-58b2-bf0b-dbaba725fc42" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}+1}{Q(x)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385280} {:answer/id #uuid "63a60a7b-ecc2-5e4a-858a-caf48320bd97" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385281}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "63a60a7b-ecc2-5e4a-858a-caf48320bd97" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385281} :answer-field/id #uuid "d4ae3c95-790e-5efb-bded-f1630a881644" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385277} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2564f937-4e1b-55d8-a807-4d2d69e01a18" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{C}{{x}^{2}+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385283} {:answer/id #uuid "9f83c1f7-9351-5f02-bca9-c83c0178d49d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{C}{{e}^{x}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385284} {:answer/id #uuid "a6f98a71-e54b-50f5-a520-00700a34623b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "C{e}^{\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385285} {:answer/id #uuid "485bf278-ab89-556a-ba05-f21c26edb3cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "C{e}^{-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385286}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "485bf278-ab89-556a-ba05-f21c26edb3cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "C{e}^{-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385286} :answer-field/id #uuid "276087f8-befa-5298-8a1a-080955d42715" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385282} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ad9ff701-1597-5963-80fd-d658b7088388" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$W(x)\\ne 0$ for all $x\\in R$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385288} {:answer/id #uuid "3376d197-15e8-50c5-a82f-7f0518430a69" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$W(x)=0$ for all $x\\in R$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385289} {:answer/id #uuid "9da40d53-dced-51c6-9ebb-9faebb28b864" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$W(x)=0$ for some but not all $x\\in R$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385290}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ad9ff701-1597-5963-80fd-d658b7088388" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$W(x)\\ne 0$ for all $x\\in R$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385288} :answer-field/id #uuid "d433757c-272d-5127-8bc9-136e8756866c" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385287}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "736a842a-a005-5f7c-a75f-80d3e3e641b7" :question/math-academy-id "q-343302" :question/problem "$$\n{y}^{″}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+1}{y}^{′}+Q(x)y=0\n$$\n\nLet ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ be solutions to the second-order homogeneous differential equation above, where $Q(x)$ is continuous on $(-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ The Wronskian satisfies $W(0)=2.$ Select the correct options in the reasoning below.\nThe Wronskian $W(x)$ satisfies the following first-order differential equation:\n\n$$\n{W}^{′}(x)={{field-1}}W(x)\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we get\n\n$$\nW={{field-2}}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nNow, using the fact that $W(0)=2,$ we obtain \n{{field-3}}." :question/worked-solution "Abel's identity says that if ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are solutions of\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+P(x){y}^{′}+Q(x)y=0,\n$$\n\nthen their Wronskian $W(x)$ satisfies\n\n$$\n{W}^{′}(x)=-P(x)W(x).\n$$\n\nIn our equation, the coefficient of ${y}^{′}$ is \n\n$$\nP(x)=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+1}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting into Abel's identity gives\n\n$$\n{W}^{′}(x)=-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+1}W(x).\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the first-order differential equation for $W:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{W}^{′} & =-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+1}W \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}W}{W} & =-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+1}\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\frac{\\text{d}W}{W} & =\\int -\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|W| & =-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x)+K \\\\ |W| & ={e}^{K}{e}^{-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x)} \\\\ W & =C{e}^{-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant.\n\nNext, we use the given value $W(0)=2$ to find $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W(0) & =C{e}^{-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(0)} \\\\ 2 & =C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have $W(x)\\ne 0\\text{ for all }x\\in R.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069268 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068861 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a7f04ab7-f6f6-5d01-bfcf-b667e37e4f0b" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Quadratic Equation When the Solutions Are Irrational"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "824db7a0-468b-5b7c-bdd3-7bd19185dd56" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p + q = 80, z =-5 ± 5\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186066779} {:answer/id #uuid "f44af2a5-1591-5f5e-93ba-40c0ddd584b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p + q = 80, z =-8 ± 3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186066780} {:answer/id #uuid "30dfca08-4199-59da-8d02-2600e0f2122b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p + q = 75, z = 5 ± 5\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186066781} {:answer/id #uuid "4a3b9749-a33c-5c57-89ac-26939f855dba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p + q = 80, z = 5 ± 5\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186066782} {:answer/id #uuid "6d135e5e-ebb4-5c27-84bf-0be68ee6c029" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p + q =-70, z = ± \\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186066783}] :answer-field/id #uuid "988361f0-82f8-5fa0-bce8-8775fead2e27" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186066778} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9eb9e11c-6e9c-5d9f-85b6-41a705579cde" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p+q=80,\\,z=-5\\pm 5\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402357} {:answer/id #uuid "23289d14-87e9-57fb-bbda-cdd305eb9ab6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p+q=-70,\\,z=\\pm \\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402358} {:answer/id #uuid "691a3cdd-d3d5-50e7-a708-646cfa31e0a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p+q=80,\\,z=-8\\pm 3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402359} {:answer/id #uuid "823c0a9e-ef4b-5a9d-a5eb-0fe274767e74" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p+q=75,\\,z=5\\pm 5\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402360} {:answer/id #uuid "ee362d99-5b0e-55c5-83d5-a9a8872163ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p+q=80,\\,z=5\\pm 5\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402361}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9eb9e11c-6e9c-5d9f-85b6-41a705579cde" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p+q=80,\\,z=-5\\pm 5\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402357} :answer-field/id #uuid "0174c191-2a23-5d0f-b8d7-9880c6f7ee41" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402356}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5c0076f5-08a5-5d4c-9789-196650ab8295" :question/math-academy-id "q-3435" :question/problem "The equation ${z}^{2}-50=-10z$ can be written in the form $(z+p{)}^{2}=q.$ Find $p+q,$ and solve the equation." :question/worked-solution "First, we move the constant term to the right-hand side, the $-10z$ term to the left-hand side, and complete the square on the left-hand side.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{z}^{2}-50 & =-10z \\\\ {z}^{2}+10z & =50 \\\\ {z}^{2}+2\\cdot 5\\cdot z & =50 \\\\ {z}^{2}+2\\cdot 5\\cdot z+{5}^{2}-{5}^{2} & =50 \\\\ (z+5{)}^{2}-{5}^{2} & =50\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we isolate the perfect square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(z+5{)}^{2}-{5}^{2} & =50 \\\\ (z+5{)}^{2} & =75\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $p=5,q=75,$ and $p+q=80.$\n\nNext, we solve the equation using the square-root method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(z+5{)}^{2} & =75 \\\\ \\sqrt{(z+5{)}^{2}} & =\\sqrt{75} \\\\ |z+5| & =\\sqrt{75} \\\\ z+5 & =\\pm 5\\sqrt{3} \\\\ z & =-5\\pm 5\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060272 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059685 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "993c8fe4-dae9-5dc4-9f94-5012f905e0ee" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Correct Quadrant for Negative Angles that Contain More Than One Full Rotation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1f1000f1-7874-54ec-a25c-a6fdd4c99c19" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186058255} {:answer/id #uuid "3d05c2d3-5f8b-520c-9698-93cd44016078" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Between quadrants II and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186058256} {:answer/id #uuid "3e675e47-ccc8-5f07-88dd-4486ea14690b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "IV" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186058257} {:answer/id #uuid "4077c45f-7284-5f8a-b112-9435133409d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186058258} {:answer/id #uuid "73d4409a-0928-587b-a8b0-c3b59584d36a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186058259}] :answer-field/id #uuid "ba6c658f-3e45-5acf-b1fd-cc6fc99efea5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186058254} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f053f6c2-d473-5f4a-9761-a34e3f8fa5eb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402370} {:answer/id #uuid "b8c1d7ef-187c-5c23-afd5-1de27fa281b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402371} {:answer/id #uuid "e2833382-6b90-5b3c-ae32-77694f3ceaef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "IV" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402372} {:answer/id #uuid "79867f36-2c63-53a2-b0d7-906e247536ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Between quadrants II and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402373} {:answer/id #uuid "7b1cfc22-b026-5ac0-aed5-a3a643bfff05" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402374}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e2833382-6b90-5b3c-ae32-77694f3ceaef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "IV" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402372} :answer-field/id #uuid "88bbe855-4ccb-5da3-a31c-9c9bb8b0b176" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402369}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "80f5801f-10e6-5373-bb84-2300dad1ed58" :question/math-academy-id "q-34403" :question/problem "The angle $\\theta =-\\frac{9\\pi }{4}$ is represented in the coordinate plane in the usual way. In which quadrant does it lie?" :question/worked-solution "First, we find an angle that's coterminal with $-\\frac{9\\pi }{4}$ by *adding* integer multiples of $2\\pi $ until we get an angle that lies in the range $[0,2\\pi ).$\n\n- $-\\frac{9\\pi }{4}+2\\pi =-\\frac{\\pi }{4}$\n\n- $-\\frac{9\\pi }{4}+2\\cdot (2\\pi )=\\frac{7\\pi }{4}\\,✓$\n\nSo the angle $-\\frac{9\\pi }{4}$ is coterminal with $\\frac{7\\pi }{4}.$ Let's draw this coterminal angle in the coordinate plane.\n\n![](images/89/891959642b0d055d852329c59be7541179dd5ae0c4182c2bec533261ce3a700d.png)\n\nWe see that $\\frac{7\\pi }{6}$ lies in quadrant IV. Therefore, $-\\frac{9\\pi }{4}$ also lies in this quadrant."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319045 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119805 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "33586620-e110-5a37-874f-a92103c5e9fb" :knowledge-point/title "Solving for One Coefficient in a Linear Combination"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "31be8d1f-3f88-58ee-bd33-1775efcf7376" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317721} {:answer/id #uuid "68f02f55-adcf-52bf-a9c0-303e07dff714" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317924} {:answer/id #uuid "8b0a721f-8c97-5595-84ac-09f5e1a54ffa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318033} {:answer/id #uuid "bb771e3e-bdfb-53b0-8d4d-91cebbd855e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318187} {:answer/id #uuid "dd9e6693-364a-5d56-8ace-d64fc0ed7b78" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318307}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bb771e3e-bdfb-53b0-8d4d-91cebbd855e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318187} :answer-field/id #uuid "f9b90697-9d45-5745-8d5a-d193273739f5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318945}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c8dd7df5-a62a-5efa-a6ef-0c480856813f" :question/math-academy-id "q-34600" :question/problem "Find $l$, if $5{a}_{1}+l{a}_{2}=b$ and\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}11 \\\\ -15 \\\\ 25 \\\\ 16\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Using the properties of addition/subtraction and scalar multiplication of vectors to isolate the $l$ term, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5{a}_{1}+l{a}_{2} & =b \\\\ 5[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]+l[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}11 \\\\ -15 \\\\ 25 \\\\ 16\\end{aligned}] \\\\ l[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}11 \\\\ -15 \\\\ 25 \\\\ 16\\end{aligned}]-5[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}] \\\\ l[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 15 \\\\ -9\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the final equation above, we can tell that \n$l=3.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186397723 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078568 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9f542453-9758-55e6-93c6-6bfd2139ec45" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Tangent Line Approximation and Classifying it as an Underestimate or Overestimate"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4d40eb58-2655-5469-940e-f80c613a366a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397725} {:answer/id #uuid "cd504e90-e5b7-56c6-964b-40f89c7b3493" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397726} {:answer/id #uuid "a07cbcd9-56d0-586c-aa21-1a80b6af5ef6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397727} {:answer/id #uuid "9fc20b50-1f58-592c-8612-6b49865b0664" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397728} {:answer/id #uuid "286353c9-a32e-56eb-8992-b6c84200e329" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397729}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a07cbcd9-56d0-586c-aa21-1a80b6af5ef6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397727} :answer-field/id #uuid "8a9687f0-3dbc-5f03-bc8c-36c768da7028" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397724} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "92b8f43d-f4d5-509d-8b12-68825b47ce0d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "overestimate" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397731} {:answer/id #uuid "ef65b10b-e65d-5550-b964-4477632fe18b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "underestimate" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397732}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "92b8f43d-f4d5-509d-8b12-68825b47ce0d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "overestimate" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397731} :answer-field/id #uuid "52b792d3-0847-501a-907c-2ebe956b9ca4" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397730}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "fb9ed8ae-2017-567d-8d5e-a0f446be0601" :question/math-academy-id "q-346720" :question/problem "The graph of the function $y=f(x)$ passes through $(0,2)$ and is concave down on the interval $(-2,2).$ If ${f}^{′}(0)=0.5,$ estimate the value of $f(0.4)$ using the tangent line approximation of $f(x)$ at $x=0$ and determine whether the approximation is an underestimate or overestimate.$\\,f(0.4)≈$ {{field-1}} and the approximation is an {{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "The tangent line approximation of $f(x)$ at the point $a=0$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(x) & =f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a) \\\\  & =f(0)+{f}^{′}(0)(x-0).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe graph of the function passes through the point $(0,2),$ so we have $f(0)=2.$ Also, we're told that ${f}^{′}(0)=0.5.$ So, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(x) & =2+0.5x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can approximate $f(0.4)$ by evaluating the tangent line approximation at $x=0.4.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0.4) & ≈L(0.4) \\\\  & =2+0.5(0.4) \\\\  & =2.2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since the function is concave down on the interval $(-2,2),$ the linear approximation $L(x)$ is above the graph near $x=0,$ and therefore we have an $\\text{overestimate}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069272 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068884 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4fafc8c8-0452-582f-b603-6f633de9a39a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Intersection Points in the Case of Two Solutions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "39f8e453-5b4e-5424-8df3-89ef36cde644" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317752} {:answer/id #uuid "3b9175b7-617f-57a3-af8d-919ed2b0db92" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317756} {:answer/id #uuid "8e503817-bf60-566b-9a9f-488b451dfc84" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318048} {:answer/id #uuid "d442f1af-21b6-52b3-b5fa-b7866251aa30" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318271} {:answer/id #uuid "e402728a-9968-5173-84f1-7ebaf6de023f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318325}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "39f8e453-5b4e-5424-8df3-89ef36cde644" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317752} :answer-field/id #uuid "8e636a51-398f-568c-9040-f35409919a73" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318739}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "a9bdc8c8-a628-5a28-9c53-49f974293c50" :question/math-academy-id "q-3471" :question/problem "The curve $y=\\frac{1}{x}$ intersects the line $4y-5x=8$ at the points $(p,q)$ and $(r,s).$ What is $p+q+r+s?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we solve for $y$ in the second curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4y-5x & =8 \\\\ 4y & =5x+8 \\\\ y & =\\frac{5x+8}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we need to solve the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}y=\\frac{1}{x} \\\\ y=\\frac{5x+8}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the equations equal to one another and solving, we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{x} & =\\frac{5x+8}{4} \\\\ 4 & =5{x}^{2}+8x \\\\ 5{x}^{2}+8x-4 & =0 \\\\ (x+2)(5x-2) & =0 \\\\ x & =-2,\\frac{2}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the $x$-coordinates of the intersection points are $x=-2$ and $x=\\frac{2}{5}.$ To find the $y$-coordinates of the intersection points, we plug these values into either curve. Let's choose the first equation.\n\n- Substituting $x=-2,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{1}{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(-2)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso one point of intersection is $(-2,-\\frac{1}{2}).$\n\n- Substituting $x=\\frac{2}{5},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{1}{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(\\frac{2}{5})} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the other point of intersection is $(\\frac{2}{5},\\frac{5}{2}).$\n\nTherefore, the points of intersection are $(-2,-\\frac{1}{2})$ and $(\\frac{2}{5},\\frac{5}{2}).$\n\nFinally, if we let $p=-2,q=-\\frac{1}{2},r=\\frac{2}{5}$ and $s=\\frac{5}{2},$ then\n\n$$\np+q+r+s=-2-\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{2}{5}+\\frac{5}{2}=\\frac{2}{5}.\n$$\n\nA sketch of the situation is shown below.\n\n![](images/d9/d995288e573a22db67c7d4c82c1a12bcbb41fd880f706b9fb62062aa90e2d5ba.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186391890 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198473 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b31f2bdc-c267-5fc7-bd0f-283777ee4fee" :knowledge-point/title "The Derivative of a Three-Component Vector-Valued Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8d8f4b31-7cd4-567a-b1ab-e1908f4c5bd0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2{e}^{\\pi }\\,i+{e}^{\\pi }\\,j-2{e}^{\\pi }\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391892} {:answer/id #uuid "770c4032-8926-5b49-96b8-17c721eb3eeb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{\\pi }\\,i-{e}^{\\pi }\\,j+{e}^{\\pi }\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391893} {:answer/id #uuid "d767d08e-9f3d-5075-804a-e32b3b238995" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{\\pi }\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391894} {:answer/id #uuid "53ba9a50-f890-5647-8a42-ad787ec3b6e8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{e}^{\\pi }\\,i+{e}^{\\pi }\\,j-{e}^{\\pi }\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391895} {:answer/id #uuid "fadb0885-54d3-518c-85c6-6d456aea62cc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{e}^{\\pi }\\,i-{e}^{\\pi }\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391896}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "53ba9a50-f890-5647-8a42-ad787ec3b6e8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{e}^{\\pi }\\,i+{e}^{\\pi }\\,j-{e}^{\\pi }\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391895} :answer-field/id #uuid "1620165b-3bcb-5548-8049-48ba8d5cc382" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391891}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7e579ce0-d5f9-57b8-931d-2fc18fc335d1" :question/math-academy-id "q-34724" :question/problem "Find the derivative of  $f(t)={e}^{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+{e}^{t}\\,j+{e}^{t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,k$ at $t=\\pi .$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find ${f}^{′}(t)$ by differentiating each component:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t})\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,k \\\\  & =({e}^{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+{e}^{t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,i+{e}^{t}\\,j+({e}^{t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-{e}^{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to compute ${f}^{′}(\\pi )$ we substitute $t=\\pi $ into the expression for ${f}^{′}(t)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(\\pi ) & =({e}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi +{e}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi )\\,i+{e}^{\\pi }\\,j+({e}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi -{e}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi )\\,k \\\\  & =({e}^{\\pi }\\cdot 0+{e}^{\\pi }\\cdot (-1))\\,i+{e}^{\\pi }\\,j+({e}^{\\pi }\\cdot (-1)-{e}^{\\pi }\\cdot 0)\\,k \\\\  & =-{e}^{\\pi }\\,i+{e}^{\\pi }\\,j-{e}^{\\pi }\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186320740 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184212 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3202d7cc-541b-553b-8ec1-d4ea85dd0590" :knowledge-point/title "Completing the Square on an Expression With a Negative Leading Coefficient"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "53025447-2eb1-5df5-a00f-6e8c8a103513" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2{(z+\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{89}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319630} {:answer/id #uuid "66db5717-8d6c-56e3-9e6e-8159327ca744" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2{(z+\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{67}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319706} {:answer/id #uuid "6f875b91-463b-5b9e-b642-9334220d3e01" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2{(z-\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{49}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319727} {:answer/id #uuid "78093d05-d766-51e5-bfbd-15a4b1ad83e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2{(z-\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}+\\frac{67}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319753} {:answer/id #uuid "b538e599-0c29-5367-95db-1d80a51d71e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2{(z+\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}+\\frac{81}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319948}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b538e599-0c29-5367-95db-1d80a51d71e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2{(z+\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}+\\frac{81}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319948} :answer-field/id #uuid "9c1dc974-537c-5a19-8b0e-de4ef3bf427a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320478}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "8b2f4df1-3e4e-5541-bb2f-d2b7890aaf73" :question/math-academy-id "q-35079" :question/problem "Rewrite $-2{z}^{2}-14z+16$ by completing the square." :question/worked-solution "Notice that the leading coefficient is $-2.$ So, we take out a factor of $-2$ from the entire expression:\n\n$$\n-2({z}^{2}+7z-8)\n$$\n\nLet's now complete the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n{z}^{2}+7z-8\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{z}^{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{7}{2}\\cdot z-8\n$$\n\nThen, we *add* and *then subtract* ${(\\frac{7}{2})}^{2},$ writing the constant term $8$ last:\n\n$$\n{{z}^{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{7}{2}\\cdot z+{(\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}}_{⏟}-{(\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}-8\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n{(z+\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}-{(\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}-8\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n{(z+\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{81}{4}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression::\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2({z}^{2}+7z-8) & =-2({(z+\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{81}{4}) \\\\  & =-2{(z+\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}+(-2)\\cdot (-\\frac{81}{4}) \\\\  & =-2{(z+\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}+\\frac{81}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106285 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105854 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a5bea806-2913-57f5-ba32-be7aeda5bd15" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Limit at Infinity of a Power Function With an Odd Power"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "15ff9d00-cfe3-5000-9f39-f5b084d00080" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319455} {:answer/id #uuid "9035921e-6fcf-5772-b9a5-7d9a496b6106" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319823} {:answer/id #uuid "d6df4228-d351-594e-9eea-e865f3a883bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320046} {:answer/id #uuid "dcc970a1-4ea5-589f-8352-ce90a07452c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320063} {:answer/id #uuid "f9a74ebb-b1db-5cf0-a20e-5165336efc1a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{DNE}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320137}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d6df4228-d351-594e-9eea-e865f3a883bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320046} :answer-field/id #uuid "1cb04ca6-e219-5d5e-ab67-487f7d66d988" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320233}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7c7529a5-2ffe-53f0-9bca-cd9b5d97a802" :question/math-academy-id "q-35192" :question/problem "Evaluate ${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }(-\\frac{{x}^{5}}{4}).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we note that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }-\\frac{{x}^{5}}{4} & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }(-\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot {x}^{5}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{x}^{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe graph $y={x}^{5}$ is power function with an odd positive power and therefore has the property that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{x}^{5}=-\\infty .\n$$\n\nMultiplication by the negative constant $-\\frac{1}{4}$ changes the sign of the limit. \nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{x}^{5} & =-{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{x}^{5}=\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }(-\\frac{{x}^{5}}{4})=\\infty .\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186320960 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078416 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "40a70c8d-dc8e-52bd-a679-ffd6d1940451" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Inverse of a Function With a Parameter"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0b9b836c-fae2-50e0-a795-3a27cfdd5785" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{x+a}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319421} {:answer/id #uuid "3492000c-c2fa-51a4-bcee-24c414d9ca52" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}^{-1}(x)=7x-a" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319554} {:answer/id #uuid "8d3d6dcf-2ec9-5287-a8ef-e92f9f472281" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{x-7}{a}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319812} {:answer/id #uuid "ccb04d9c-7b29-5181-b95e-06fc65228ede" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{x+7}{a}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320017} {:answer/id #uuid "d00e46ee-e183-5993-a320-dcbaa9aa4eae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{x-a}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320030}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ccb04d9c-7b29-5181-b95e-06fc65228ede" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{x+7}{a}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320017} :answer-field/id #uuid "c5d4e30f-32ac-5460-b456-38e5a87775f1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320578}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "abfc488e-a974-5dbc-b40c-2f281295336b" :question/math-academy-id "q-35217" :question/problem "Find the inverse of the function $f(x)=ax-7$, where $a$ is a non-zero constant." :question/worked-solution "To find the inverse of a function, we follow these steps:\n\n**Step 1:** Replace $f(x)$ with $y$:\n\n$$\ny=ax-7\n$$\n\n**Step 2:** Swap $x$ and $y$:\n\n$$\nx=ay-7\n$$\n\n**Step 3:** Solve for $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =ay-7 \\\\ x+7 & =ay \\\\ \\frac{x+7}{a} & =y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4:** Replace $y$ with ${f}^{-1}(x)$:\n\n$$\n{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{x+7}{a}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186320986 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105849 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4974097a-95c9-5835-ba1d-46480efab66b" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Limit at Infinity of a Power Function With an Even Power"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3ccc7bce-8234-5ee1-bd8b-2bcdd3fcbd07" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319574} {:answer/id #uuid "574ba857-9f96-590d-abdc-bb096d802847" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319648} {:answer/id #uuid "ac7a7fe3-5133-5ba3-baf2-2c6b2e00ce1a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{DNE}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319916} {:answer/id #uuid "e1c8a647-b5b3-5af4-85b7-661bdc068c81" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320074} {:answer/id #uuid "f8b296d9-adc9-527b-a4b4-ccaabe649e19" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320133}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3ccc7bce-8234-5ee1-bd8b-2bcdd3fcbd07" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319574} :answer-field/id #uuid "5e14ff5e-6a4f-5e24-8876-c8a24ddf7181" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320361}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "1ffa1d84-420d-51e5-b5d5-2102976566da" :question/math-academy-id "q-35243" :question/problem "![](images/67/67b77d2d3798e87593c7dddeea93603fff36d9153f53248cc2dab295f4879321.png)\n\nThe figure above shows the graph of $f(x).$ Find ${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{{x}^{2}}{f(x)}.$" :question/worked-solution "Applying the product rule and the quotient rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{{x}^{2}}{f(x)} & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }({x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{f(x)}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{x}^{2}\\cdot ({lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{1}{f(x)}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{({lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }f(x))}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the graph, we get that ${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }f(x)=3.$ Therefore, the above becomes:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{{x}^{2}}{f(x)} & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{x}^{2}\\cdot (\\frac{1}{3}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{x}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe graph $y={x}^{2}$ is an upward parabola, so\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{x}^{2}=\\infty .\n$$\n\nMultiplication by the positive constant $\\frac{1}{3}$ does not change the sign of the limit. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }(\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{2})={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }({x}^{2})=\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078876 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078416 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "40a70c8d-dc8e-52bd-a679-ffd6d1940451" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Inverse of a Function With a Parameter"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "28a8e3f3-e6e5-59bd-9144-f9733223829f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}^{-1}(x)=ax-11a" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319519} {:answer/id #uuid "8ee8eb52-9b9f-56f1-9ecb-13190d21a579" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}^{-1}(x)=ax-11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319819} {:answer/id #uuid "b056fbea-f31f-565e-a6ee-a22a4a856c27" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}^{-1}(x)=ax+11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319927} {:answer/id #uuid "b321e6e4-fc1e-54d2-9a1a-305a18b80a61" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}^{-1}(x)=ax+11a" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319939} {:answer/id #uuid "b495768c-d594-5351-a058-aad82b451854" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}^{-1}(x)=x+11a" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319945}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "28a8e3f3-e6e5-59bd-9144-f9733223829f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}^{-1}(x)=ax-11a" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319519} :answer-field/id #uuid "1f5fa981-8f9f-5e72-b3b9-955186ce41e6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320236}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "40530ca3-e238-5640-94b0-1433afa01d34" :question/math-academy-id "q-35310" :question/problem "Find the inverse of the function $f(x)=\\frac{x}{a}+11$, where $a$ is a non-zero constant." :question/worked-solution "To find the inverse of a function, we follow these steps:\n\n**Step 1:** Replace $f(x)$ with $y$:\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{x}{a}+11\n$$\n\n**Step 2:** Swap $x$ and $y$:\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{y}{a}+11\n$$\n\n**Step 3:** Solve for $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{y}{a}+11 \\\\ x-11 & =\\frac{y}{a} \\\\ a(x-11) & =y \\\\ ax-11a & =y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4:** Replace $y$ with ${f}^{-1}(x)$:\n\n$$\n{f}^{-1}(x)=ax-11a\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377568 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078599 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fb30dbbf-4f83-59d0-b712-f0e592aafb27" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Definite Integral of a Piecewise Curved Function Given Its Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a0548f28-a84c-5788-9dd5-1ea59549840c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6+4\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377570} {:answer/id #uuid "807dad30-2355-54f9-8046-27068d7ea408" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12+2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377571} {:answer/id #uuid "f8ec02d2-f9a0-5fad-ba9d-4e446fcc9661" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12+4\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377572} {:answer/id #uuid "12ea0440-97a1-5184-9d99-be591e1d199a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6+2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377573} {:answer/id #uuid "0a2f2dcd-e4d2-5a84-bc71-1dbf0587c8ce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12+3\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377574}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "807dad30-2355-54f9-8046-27068d7ea408" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12+2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377571} :answer-field/id #uuid "59c92177-9ded-5c63-9cb3-4c4f4a2d25b9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377569}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6566d8aa-091d-5756-ad52-e9f54384a0e9" :question/math-academy-id "q-353388" :question/problem "![](images/01/017fd41508a91d5674af92b280fd73690357645428279e09e8633fe70ce3278d.png)\n\nGiven the graph of $y=f(x)$ above, find the value of \n${\\int }_{-3}^{5}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "The graph consists of $2$ parts: the line segment for $x\\in [-3,1)$ and the arc of the semi-circle for $x\\in [1,5].$ So, we can use the additive property of integrals to get\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-3}^{5}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={{{\\int }_{-3}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{1}}+{{{\\int }_{1}^{5}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{2}}.\n$$\n\n- The $1$st integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [-3,1).$ The corresponding region is a rectangle, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =b\\cdot h \\\\  & =4\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =12.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The $2$nd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [1,5].$ The corresponding region is a semi-circle, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\pi {r}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\pi \\cdot (2)^{2} \\\\  & =2\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-3}^{5}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={A}_{1}+{A}_{2} \\\\  & =12+2\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
